#{[17592186192622 :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ be the probability that a randomly selected customer buys at least one muffin. Then, we have the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {H}_{0} : p & \\displaystyle  = 55 \\mathrm{\\% } \\\\ \\displaystyle {H}_{1} : p & \\displaystyle  \\ne  55 \\mathrm{\\% }\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThe critical region is the set of values of $X$ for which we reject the null hypothesis. A critical value is the boundary point of the critical region, and it is included in the region.\n\nThis is a two-sided test. So, to reject the null hypothesis, we need to observe an abnormally large or abnormally small value of $X$.\n\nTo reject the null hypothesis at a significance level of $10\\%$, we must observe a value $x$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X \\leq x) = 0.05\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq x) = 0.05\n$$\n\nunder the null hypothesis.\n\nUsing the table, we have the following probabilities:\n\n- For the left tail:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 0) & =P(X=0) \\\\  & =0.0083 \\\\  & <0.05\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ P(X\\le 1) & =0.0692 \\\\  & ≮0.05\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the right tail:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 6) &= 1 - P(X \\le 5) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.9723 \\\\\n&= 0.0277 \\\\\n&< 0.05✓ \\\\\nP(X \\ge 5) &= 1 - P(X \\le 4) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.8364 \\\\\n&= 0.1636 \\\\\n&= ≮0.05\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the values of $X$ that would cause the null hypothesis to be rejected at a significance level of $10\\%$ are the values such that\n\n$$\nX\\leq 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nX\\geq 6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the critical values are $0$ and $6$."] [17592186193114 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={t}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=t\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $t\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={t}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=3{\\int }_{\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u=-3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/c8/c870dd367332455f2d4c35f85a188c64385a5195ca1058405a93f84c4ce31fd1.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{6t}{{t}^{4}+3}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right)]-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186193232 :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={e}^{x}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u={e}^{x}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={e}^{x}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{1}{1+3{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}u)]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/f5/f55b37b456a6ab60c495c64be5f7afe510dc0889890ed2a070b371f9bbe69c1a.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+3{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\lim_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}a)]-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186193369 :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{k}\\right)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{3}\\right)]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\n![](images/24/2465f099a94aa966dbf33eb89aa00973e692fa97c887a9b79baff8260f5d31cd.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{{z}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{3}\\right)] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186193816 :tutorial/content "Let's return to our first example, where we wish to conduct a hypothesis test to determine whether a particular coin is unfair.\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- We defined $p$ as \"the probability that the coin lands on heads when tossed randomly.\"\n- Since we want to check whether the coin is unfair, we have the following null and alternative hypotheses:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{0}:p & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {H}_{1}:p & \\ne \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The significance level is $\\alpha = 5\\%$.\n\nUnder the conditions specified in the null hypothesis, the random variable $X$ is a binomial random variable given by\n\n$$\nX\\sim B\\left(10,\\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nThe probability mass function of $X$ under the null hypothesis is shown below.\n\n![](images/79/79306d39f30f044beb30aaa7f6356b7a226c0f3958597d2e230601d51474b801.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When we select a significance level $\\alpha$ in a two-tailed test, the convention is to allow $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ at either tail.\n- So if $\\alpha = 5\\%$, we have $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2} = 2.5\\%$ at either tail.\n\nNow, suppose we conduct our experiment and get $2$ heads out of $10$ tosses.\n\nIf the result $X=2$ is statistically significant, it will lie in the critical region in the *left* tail of the probability distribution of $f(x)$.\n\nWe can compute the probability of getting\n\n$$\nX\\leq 2\n$$\n\nheads under the null hypothesis using our knowledge of the binomial distribution:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( X \\le  2 ) & \\displaystyle  = P ( X = 0 ) + P ( X = 1 ) + P ( X = 2 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0} \\right) {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{0} {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10 - 0} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1} \\right) {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{1} {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10 - 1} + \\left( \\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{2} \\right) {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{2} {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10 - 2} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 1 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} + 10 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} + 45 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 56 \\cdot  {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{10} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.055 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 5.5 \\mathrm{\\% } .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nThis probability is *larger than*\n\n$$\n\\dfrac\\alpha2 = 2.5\\%\n$$\n\nThis means that our result is *not* statistically significant. In other words, it is \"likely enough\" that this event could have occurred if the null hypothesis is true.\n\nTherefore, we should *not* reject the null hypothesis. Consequently, we conclude that the coin is fair."] [17592186193831 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to use Riemann sums to approximate areas under curves of composite functions. Our final task is determining whether these approximations overestimate or underestimate the true areas.\n\nWe start by considering *right* Riemann sums. Recall the following facts:\n\n- If a function is strictly *increasing*, the right Riemann sum *overestimates* the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n- If a function is strictly *decreasing*, the right Riemann sum *underestimates* the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n\nLet $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x \\in [1,2]$, and differentiable for $x \\in (1,2)$. Now, consider the function\n\n$$\ng(x) = x^2 \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nSuppose we use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve $y = g(x)$ over $x\\in [1,2]$. Does the right Riemann sum overestimate or underestimate the true area?\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have $f'(x) \\gt 0$.\n- Differentiating our expression for $g(x)$ using the product rule, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{'}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{'}\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}^{'}(x) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}^{'}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\nWe now examine the sign of each factor in this expression to determine the sign of $g'(x)$.Since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(1,2)$, we have\n$2x \\gt 0, \\qquad f(x) \\gt 0, \\qquad x^2 \\gt 0, \\qquad f'(x) \\gt 0$.\nHence, we obtain\n$$\n\\displaystyle g'(x) = \\underbrace{2x}_{\\gt 0} \\cdot \\underbrace{f(x)}_{\\gt 0} + \\underbrace{x^2}_{> 0} \\cdot \\underbrace{f'(x)}_{\\gt 0} = \\underbrace{2x \\cdot f(x)}_{\\gt 0} + \\underbrace{x^2 \\cdot f'(x)}_{\\gt 0} \\gt 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $g'(x) \\gt 0$, the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the right Riemann sum *overestimates* the area under the curve $y = g(x)$.\n\nLet's now look at an example involving a left Riemann sum."] [17592186193855 :tutorial/content "We wish to find the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\vert x + 1 \\vert = 2x - 4\n$$\n\nFor this equation to be true, either the positive or negative of the expression inside the absolute value must be equal to the right-hand side of the equation.\n\nTherefore, we have the following two cases:\n\n$$\n|x+1| = x+1 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad |x+1| = -(x+1)\n$$\n\nWe now consider these two cases separately:\n\n- If $\\mid x + 1|=x + 1$, we obtain the following equation:\n$x+1 = 2x-4$\nWe now solve this equation using the usual methods:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =2x-4 \\\\ 1 & =x-4 \\\\ 5 & =x \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1| & =2x-4 \\\\ |5+1| & =2(5)-4 \\\\ |6| & =10-4 \\\\ 6 & =6\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $x=5$ satisfies the original equation, it's a valid solution.\n- If $\\mid x + 1|=-(x + 1)$, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1| & =2x-4 \\\\ -(x+1) & =2x-4 \\\\ -x-1 & =2x-4 \\\\ -1 & =3x-4 \\\\ 3 & =3x \\\\ 1 & =x \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis *potential* solution is valid only if it solves the original equation. So, let's plug it back in:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+1| & =2x-4 \\\\ |1+1| & =2(1)-4 \\\\ |2| & =2-4 \\\\ 2 & =-2\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $x=1$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, it's *not* a valid solution. It's called an **extraneous** solution.\n\nTherefore, the *only* solution of our equation is $x = 5$. This solution corresponds to the point $P$, shown below.\n\n![](images/9b/9bed877b267808bd54f0cdc43db51264f9e94b41345da67a59fed461d77d4504.png)\n\n**Watch out!** Whenever we want to solve an absolute value equation where the variable is on both sides, we must *always* check that the solutions satisfy the original equation.\n\nSome absolute value equations have no solutions at all! Let's see an example."] [17592186193930 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to solve division problems with remainders without using models.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following division problem:\n\n$$\n30 \\div 7\n$$\n\nWe solve this division problem by finding the quotient and remainder separately:\n\n- To find the quotient, we need to determine how many times $7$ goes into $30$. To do that, we list the multiples of $7$ until we get to one that's larger than $30{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\times 7 & =7 \\\\ 2\\times 7 & =14 \\\\ 3\\times 7 & =21 \\\\ {\\color{red}{4}}\\times 7 & =28\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ 5\\times 7 & =35\\hspace{2em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nNotice that $5 \\times 7=35$ is larger than $30$, but ${\\color{red}{4}} \\times 7 = 28$ is smaller than $30$. So, $7$ goes into $30$ a total of ${\\color{red}{4}}$ times. Therefore, the quotient of our division problem is ${\\color{red}{4}}{:}$\n$30 \\div 7 = {\\color{red}{4}}\\,\\,\\textrm R\\,\\,\\bbox[3pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\color{blue}?}$\n- To find the remainder, we write the last equation as a multiplication plus the remainder:\n$30 = 7 \\times {\\color{red}{4}} \\,\\,+ \\,\\,\\bbox[3pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\color{blue}?}$\nThis equation simplifies as follows:\n$30 = 28\\,\\,+ \\,\\,\\bbox[3pt, white, border: 1px solid black]{\\color{blue}?}$\nTherefore, the remainder must be ${\\color{blue}{2}}$.\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n30 \\div 7 = {\\color{red}4} \\, \\textrm{R} \\, {\\color{blue}2}\n$$"] [17592186193947 :tutorial/content "The pooled variance $S_p^2$ is given by\n\n$$\nS_p^2 = \\dfrac{(n_1-1)S_1^2 + (n_2-1)S_2^2}{n_1+n_2-2}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $S_p^2$ is an *unbiased* estimator of $\\sigma^2$. We'll prove this later in the lesson.\n- The pooled variance extends naturally to three independent random samples with population variance $\\sigma^2$.\n$$\n\\displaystyle S_p^2 = \\dfrac{(n_1-1)S_1^2 + (n_2-1)S_2^2 + (n_3-1)S_3^2}{n_1+n_2 + n_3-3}\n$$\n\n- Note that when the samples are equal, i.e., $n_1=n_2=n$, the formula reduces to the average of the sample variances:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{p}^{2} & =\\frac{({n}_{1}-1){S}_{1}^{2}+({n}_{2}-1){S}_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{1}+{n}_{2}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n-1){S}_{1}^{2}+(n-1){S}_{2}^{2}}{2n-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n-1)({S}_{1}^{2}+{S}_{2}^{2})}{2(n-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{S}_{1}^{2}+{S}_{2}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSimilarly, for three random samples with equal sample sizes, we have\n$S_p^2 = \\dfrac{S_1^2+S_2^2 + S_3^2}{3}$.\n\n- Similar to Bessel's correction for the sample variance, the denominator $(n_1+n_2 - 2)$ guarantees that $S_p^2$ is unbiased.\n- More technically, the denominator represents our combined sample's **degrees of freedom**. The number of degrees of freedom is the number of *independent* elements in the sample.The degrees of freedom concept is tricky, so let's break it down in this case: The number of elements in the first sample is $n_1$. However, for a fixed $\\overline{X}$, only $(n_1 - 1)$ of these elements may vary freely. In other words, if the first $(n_1-1)$ elements are free to vary yet $\\overline{X}$ is fixed, this fixes the last sample element (it is *not* free to vary because the sum of all elements divided by $n_1$ must equal $\\overline X$). Thus, the number of independent sample elements in the first sample is $(n_1-1)$.By the same argument, the number of independent sample elements in the second sample is $(n_2-1)$.Thus, the total number of independent sample elements is $(n_1-1) +(n_2-1) = n_1+n_2 - 2$.You'll learn more about degrees of freedom in future lessons."] [17592186198087 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- An integer is *even* if it can be written as $2p$, where $p$ is an integer.\n- An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2q + 1$, where $q$ is an integer.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nnis an integer⇒n^{2} - n + 7is odd\n$$\n\nThe integers split naturally into even and odd numbers, and these two cases cover all possible integers. Therefore, it suffices to prove the following:\n\n$$\n(nis even)∨(nis odd)⇒n^{2} - n + 7is odd\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof by assuming $n$ is an integer and state that we'll proceed using cases.\n\n> Let $n$ be an integer. We consider the following cases:\n\nFirst, we consider the case when $n$ is even.\n\n> **Case 1**. If $n$ is even, then $n = 2a$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression $n^2-n+7$ and show it is odd by writing it as $1$ more than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> As a result, we obtain\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-n+7 & =(2a)^{2}-(2a)+7 \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}-2a+7 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}-2a+6)+1 \\\\  & =2(2{a}^{2}-a+3)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> Let $b = 2a^2 - a + 3$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> So, $n^2-n+7 = 2b+1$, which means that it is odd.\n\nNow, we consider the case when $n$ is odd.\n\n> **Case 2**. If $n$ is odd, then $n = 2a+1$ for some integer $a$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute\n\n$$\nn = 2a + 1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $n^2-n+7$ and show it is odd by writing it as $1$ more than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> As a result, we obtain\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-n+7 & =(2a+1)^{2}-(2a+1)+7 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a+1)-(2a+1)+7 \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}+2a+7 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+2a+6)+1 \\\\  & =2(2{a}^{2}+a+3)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> Let $b = 2a^2 + a + 3$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> So, $n^2-n+7 = 2b+1$, which means it is odd.\n\nFinally, we combine both cases:\n\n> Combining the cases above, we can conclude that $n^2-n+7$ is odd for any integer $n$."] [17592186198135 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cup C & =\\{1,2,3,5\\}, \\\\ B\\cup C & =\\{1,2,3,4\\}, \\\\ A\\cap C & =\\{1\\}, \\\\ B\\cap C & =\\{2\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\ncalculate $(A \\cap B) \\cup C$ and\n$(A \\cup B) \\cap C$."] [17592186198143 :question/worked-solution "A *null sequence* is a sequence that converges to zero:\n\n- Sequence I is a null sequence. By dividing the numerator and denominator by $n^2$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{a}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}\\left(\\frac{n}{{n}^{2}+1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}\\left(\\frac{\\left(\\frac{n}{{n}^{2}}\\right)}{(\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}\\right) \\\\  & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}\\left(\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{n}\\right)}{(1+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1+0} \\\\  & =0\\hspace{1em}{\\color{darkgreen}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is *not* a null sequence since\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{b}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}\\sqrt{n+1} \\\\  & =\\mathrm{\\infty } \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Sequence III is null. Notice that this is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r =\\dfrac{8}{9}$.\nSince $\\mid r \\mid < 1$, the sequence converges to zero:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{c}_{n} & =\\lim_{n\\to \\mathrm{\\infty }}{\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)}^{n} \\\\  & =0\\hspace{1em}{\\color{darkgreen}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186198147 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} = 0\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n\\forall \\varepsilon > 0, \\: \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\: n \\geq N \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nWriting this in words, we have:\n\n> We need to show that for any real $\\varepsilon > 0$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $\\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon$.\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $\\varepsilon$.\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\gt 0\n$$\n\nfor any natural\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\right| = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for $n$:\n\n> Solving this inequality for $n$, we get\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} & <ε \\\\ 1 & <ε\\sqrt{n} \\\\ 1 & <n{ε}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{ε}^{2}} & <n \\\\ n & >\\frac{1}{{ε}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\right| < \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nit's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\varepsilon^2}\n$$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\n> Therefore, we choose $N > \\dfrac{1}{\\varepsilon^2}$.\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} \\\\  & <\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1/{ε}^{2}}} \\\\  & =ε.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} = 0.\n$$"] [17592186198171 :question/worked-solution "A set $A$ is *countably infinite* (or *denumerable*) if\n\n$$\n|A| = |\\mathbb{N}|.\n$$\n\nA set is *countable* if it is either finite or countably infinite.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $A$ has infinitely many elements.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\{(-2)^{n}∣n\\in \\mathbb{N}\\} \\\\  & =\\{-2,4,-8,16,-32…\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\ncan be represented as a (one-way infinite) sequence. So, it's countably infinite (or denumerable).\n- Statement III is true since any countably infinite set is also countable.\n- Statement IV is false since statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186198178 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} (4-n^2) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n\\forall M < 0, \\: \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\: n \\geq N \\: \\Rightarrow \\: (4-n^2) \\lt M\n$$\n\nLet's write this in words.\n\n> We need to show that for any real $M < 0$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $4-n^2 < M$.\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $M$.\n\nFirst, notice that $4-n^2 < 0$ for any\n\n$$\nn \\geq 3\n$$\n\nSo, we solve the inequality $4-n^2 < M$ for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 3{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-{n}^{2} & <M \\\\ -{n}^{2} & <M-4 \\\\ {n}^{2} & >4-M \\\\ n & >\\sqrt{4-M}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want $4-n^2 < M$, it's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than $\\sqrt{4-M}$.\n\nNotice that, since $M < 0$, we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{4 - M} > \\sqrt{4} = 2\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nN\\geq 3\n$$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\n> Suppose $M < 0$. Let's choose some natural number\n> $N > \\sqrt{4-M}$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the sequence's $n$th term:\n\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =4-{n}^{2} \\\\  & <4-(\\sqrt{4-M})^{2} \\\\  & <4-4+M \\\\  & =M.\\end{aligned}$.\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} (4-n^2) = -\\infty.\n$$"] [17592186198191 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} \\right)= -1\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n\\forall \\varepsilon > 0, \\: \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\: n \\geq N \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\left| \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} -(-1) \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nIn other words, we need to show that for any real $\\varepsilon \\gt 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number\n\n$$\nn \\geq N\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} -(-1) \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $\\varepsilon$.\n\nFirst, we simplifying $\\mid a_n - L \\mid$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{a}_{n}-L| & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}-10}-(-1)| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}-10}+1| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}-10}+\\frac{{4}^{n}-10}{{4}^{n}-10}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}+{4}^{n}-10}{{4}^{n}-10}| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{9}{{4}^{n}-10}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{9}{{4}^{n}-10}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{4^n-10} \\gt 0\n$$\n\nfor any natural\n\n$$\nn \\geq 2\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n\\mid a_n - L \\mid = \\left| \\dfrac{9}{4^n-10} \\right| = \\dfrac{9}{4^n-10}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $\\mid a_n - L \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for\n\n$$\nn\\geq 2{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{{4}^{n}-10} & <ε \\\\ 9 & <ε({4}^{n}-10) \\\\ \\frac{9}{ε} & <{4}^{n}-10 \\\\ 10+\\frac{9}{ε} & <{4}^{n} \\\\ {log}_{4}(10+\\frac{9}{ε}) & <n \\\\ n & >{log}_{4}(10+\\frac{9}{ε}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want\n\n$$\n\\left| \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} - (-1) \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nit's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than\n\n$$\n\\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\n> Suppose $\\varepsilon > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right)$.\n\nNotice that, since\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon} > 0\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right) > \\log_4(10)\\approx 1.66\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\nN \\geq 2\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{1-{4}^{n}}{{4}^{n}-10}-(-1)| & =\\frac{9}{{4}^{n}-10} \\\\  & <\\frac{9}{{4}^{{log}_{4}(10+9/ε)}-10} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{(10+\\frac{9}{ε}-10)} \\\\  & =ε.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, according to the definition,\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} \\right)= -1\n$$\n\nThe full proof is given below:\n\n> Suppose $\\varepsilon > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\log_4\\left(10+\\dfrac{9}{\\varepsilon}\\right)$.\n> Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\mid \\frac{1 - 4^{n}}{4^{n} - 10} - (-1) \\mid \\mid < (9)/(4^{\\log_{4} (10 + 9/ε)} - 10); = (9)/((10 + \\frac{9}{ε} - 10)); = ε.\n$$\n> Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1-4^n}{4^n-10} = -1.\n$$"] [17592186198201 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nm \\textrm{is odd and} n \\textrm{is even} \\Rightarrow m - n \\textrm{is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $m$ is odd and $n$ is even.\n\n> Let $m$ and $n$ be integers, where $m$ is odd and $n$ is even. Then, $m=2a+1$ and $n=2b$ for some integers $a$ and $b$.\n\n**Watch Out**! We must use two *different* integers to represent the oddness of $m$ and the evenness of $n$. In this case, we use $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute\n\n$$\nm=2a+1\n$$\n\nand $n=2b$ into our expression $m - n$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2$, plus $1$.\n\n> Therefore, we obtain\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}m-n & =(2a+1)-(2b) \\\\  & =2a+1-2b \\\\  & =(2a-2b)+1 \\\\  & =2\\underbrace{(a-b)}_{c}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $c=a - b$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nm - n = 2c+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $m - n$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $c=a - b$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore,\n> $\\begin{aligned}m-n & =2\\underbrace{(a-b)}_{c}+1 \\\\  & =2c+1\\end{aligned}$\n> which is one more than a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, we conclude that $m - n$ is odd."] [17592186198213 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n(m-1)n^2 \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m \\text{is even and} n \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\textrm{is even and} n \\textrm{is odd} \\right) \\Rightarrow \\neg\\left((m-1)n^2\\textrm{is odd}\\right)\n$$\n\nNegating the right-hand-side of our implication is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\textrm{is even and} n \\textrm{is odd} \\right) \\Rightarrow (m-1)n^2 \\textrm{is even}\n$$\n\nTo negate the statement \"$m$ is even and $n$ is odd\", we use De Morgan's law for conjunctions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n¬(mis even andnis odd) &= ¬(mis even∧nis odd) \\\\\n&= ¬(mis even)∨¬(nis odd) \\\\\n&= mis odd∨nis even \\\\\n&= mis odd ornis even\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's write down the contrapositive of our statement.\n\n> The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n> $m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (m-1)n^2 \\text{is even}$\n> We'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nNow, we have two cases: $m$ is odd, or $n$ is even.\n\n- First, we consider the case when $m$ is odd.\n\n> **Case 1**. If $m$ is odd, then $m=2a+1$ for some integer $a$.\n> As a result, we obtain\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}(m-1){n}^{2} & =(2a+1-1){n}^{2} \\\\  & =2a{n}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\underbrace{(a{n}^{2})}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> Let $b=an^2$. Since $a$ and $n$ are integers, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\begin{aligned}(m-1){n}^{2} & =2\\underbrace{(a{n}^{2})}_{b} \\\\  & =2b,\\end{aligned}$,\n> which is a multiple of $2$. Hence, $(m-1)n^2$ is even.\n\n- Now, we consider the case when $n$ is even.\n\n> **Case 2**. If $n$ is even, then $n=2a$ for some integer $a$.\n> As a result, we obtain\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}(m-1){n}^{2} & =(m-1)(2a)^{2} \\\\  & =4(m-1){a}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\underbrace{(2(m-1){a}^{2})}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> Let $b = 2(m-1)a^2$. Since $a$ and $m$ are integers, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\begin{aligned}(m-1){n}^{2} & =2\\underbrace{(2(m-1){a}^{2})}_{b} \\\\  & =2b,\\end{aligned}$,\n> which is a multiple of $2$. Hence, $(m-1)n^2$ is even.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, combining the cases above, we conclude that $(m-1)n^2$ is odd only if $m$ is even and $n$ is odd."] [17592186198232 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nm \\text{is even and} n \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is even}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $m$ is even and $n$ is odd.\n\n> Let $m$ and $n$ be integers, where $m$ is even and $n$ is odd. Then, $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$ for some integers $a$ and $b$.\n\n**Watch Out**! We must use two *different* integers to represent the evenness of $m$ and the oddness of $n$. In this case, we use $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $m=2a$ and\n\n$$\nn=2b+1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $mn$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\begin{aligned}mn & =(2a)\\cdot (2b+1) \\\\  & =4ab+2a \\\\  & =2\\underbrace{(2ab+a)}_{c}.\\end{aligned}$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nc=2ab+a\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that $mn = 2c$, which shows that $mn$ is indeed a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $c=2ab+a$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore,\n> $\\begin{aligned}mn & =2\\underbrace{(2ab+a)}_{c} \\\\  & =2c\\end{aligned}$\n> which is a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, we conclude that $mn$ is even."] [17592186198233 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p$.\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1$.\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nmn \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is odd}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\neg\\left(mn \\text{is odd}\\right)\n$$\n\nNegating the right-hand-side of our implication is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is odd or} n \\text{is odd}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn\\text{is even}\n$$\n\nTo negate the statement \"$m$ is odd or $n$ is odd\", we use De Morgan's law for disjunctions.\n\n$$\n¬(mis odd ornis odd) \\mid ⇔¬(mis odd∨nis odd); ⇔¬(mis odd)∧¬(nis odd); ⇔mis even∧nis even; ⇔mis even andnis even\n$$\n\nSo, let's write down the contrapositive of our original statement.\n\n> The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:\n> $m \\text{is even and} n \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is even}$\n> We'll prove this statement instead of the original one.\n\nWe start by using the fact that $m$ and $n$ are even.\n\n> If $m$ and $n$ are even, then\n> $m = 2a \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = 2b$,\n> where $a$ and $b$ are integers.\n\nWe substitute $m=2a$ and $n=2b$ into the expression $mn$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Therefore,\n> $\\begin{aligned}mn & =(2a)(2b) \\\\  & =4ab \\\\  & =2\\underbrace{(2ab)}_{c}.\\end{aligned}$.\n\nNow, if we define another integer $c=2ab$ (highlighted above), we have that $mn= 2c$, which shows that $mn$ is indeed a multiple of $2$.\n\n> Let $c=2ab$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\begin{aligned}mn & =2\\underbrace{(2ab)}_{c} \\\\  & =2c,\\end{aligned}$,\n> which is a multiple of $2$.\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $mn$ is even."] [17592186198849 :question/problem "The notation $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{n \\to  \\infty}\\left(\\frac{n^{2} + n}{2n^{2} - 1}\\right) = \\frac{1}{2}\n$$ means the following:\n\n{{field-1}}\n$ε > 0$,\n{{field-2}}\n$N ∈ N$,\n{{field-3}}\n$n ∈ N$,\n{{field-4}}\n$⇒ \\mid \\frac{n^{2} + n}{2n^{2} - 1} -$\n{{field-5}}\n$\\mid$\n{{field-6}}."] [17592186198932 :question/problem "Given that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\times C & =\\{(1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4)\\}, \\\\ B\\times C & =\\{(2,3),(2,4),(4,3),(4,4),(6,3),(6,4)\\},\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhat is $(A∖B) \\cdot C$?"] [17592186199580 :tutorial/content "To show that a statement is true, we might need to break the proof up into several cases. This method is sometimes called **proof by cases**.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's prove the following proposition:\n\n$$\nnis an integer⇒n^{2} - 3nis even\n$$\n\nThe integers partition naturally into even and odd numbers, and these two cases cover all possible integers.\n\n$$\n\\mathbb Z = \\big\\{0, \\pm 2, \\pm 4, \\ldots\\big\\} \\cup \\big\\{\\pm 1, \\pm 3, \\pm 5, \\ldots\\big\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it suffices to prove the following:\n\n$$\n(nis even)∨(nis odd)⇒n^{2} - 3nis even\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof by assuming $n$ is an integer and state that we'll proceed using cases.\n\n> *Let $n$ be an integer. We consider the following cases:*\n\nFirst, we consider the case when $n$ is even.\n\n> ***Case 1.*** *If $n$ is even, then $n = 2a$ for some integer $a$.*\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression $n^2-3n$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *As a result, we obtain*\n> $\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-3n & =(2a)^{2}-3(2a) \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}-6a \\\\  & =2(2{a}^{2}-3a).\\end{aligned}$.\n> *Let $b = 2a^2 - 3a$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.*\n> *So, $n^2-3n = 2b$, which means that it is even.*\n\nNow, we consider the case when $n$ is odd.\n\n> ***Case 2.*** *If $n$ is odd, then $n = 2a+1$ for some integer $a$.*\n\nThe idea is to substitute\n\n$$\nn = 2a + 1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $n^2-3n$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *As a result, we obtain*\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-3n & =(2a+1)^{2}-3(2a+1) \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a+1)-3(2a+1) \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}-2a-2 \\\\  & =2(2{a}^{2}-a-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> *Let $b = 2a^2 - a - 1$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.*\n> *So, $n^2-3n = 2b$, which means it is even*\n\nFinally, we combine both cases:\n\n> *Combining the cases above, we can conclude that $n^2-3n$ is even for any integer $n$.*\n\nFor clarity, let's restate the proposition and full proof."] [17592186199588 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following proposition.\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{mn \\text{is even}}_{P(m,n)} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\underbrace{m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}}_{Q(m,n)}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P \\Rightarrow Q$, it suffices to prove the contrapositive\n\n$$\n\\neg Q \\Rightarrow \\neg P\n$$\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad \\neg\\left(mn \\text{is even}\\right)\n$$\n\nNegating the right-hand-side of our implication is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\neg\\left(m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}\\right) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is odd}\n$$\n\nTo negate the statement \"$m$ is even or $n$ is even,\" we use De Morgan's law for disjunctions.\n\n$$\n¬(mis even ornis even) \\mid ⇔¬(mis even∨nis even); ⇔¬(mis even)∧¬(nis even); ⇔mis odd∧nis odd; ⇔mis odd andnis odd\n$$\n\nSo, let's write down the contrapositive of our original statement.\n\n> *The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:*\n> $m \\text{is odd and} n \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is odd}$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $m$ and $n$ are odd.\n\n> *If $m$ and $n$ are odd, then*\n> $m = 2a+1 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = 2b+1$,\n> *where $a$ and $b$ are integers.*\n\nWe substitute\n\n$$\nm=2a+1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn=2b+1\n$$\n\ninto the expression $mn$ and show it is odd by writing it as one larger than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Therefore,*\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}mn & =(2a+1)(2b+1) \\\\  & =4ab+2a+2b+1 \\\\  & =2\\underbrace{(2ab+a+b)}_{c}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nc=2ab+a+b\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nmn = 2c+1\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $mn$ is indeed one larger than a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Let $c=2ab+a+b$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.*\n> *Hence, we have*\n> $\\begin{aligned}mn & =2\\underbrace{(2ab+a+b)}_{c}+1 \\\\  & =2c+1,\\end{aligned}$,\n> *which is one larger than a multiple of $2$.*\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\n> *Therefore, $mn$ is odd.*\n\nFor clarity, let's restate the proposition and its full proof."] [17592186199605 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the null geometric sequence\n\n$$\na_n = \\left(\\dfrac23\\right)^n, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nEarlier, we showed that if we select\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon = 0.000\\, 4\n$$\n\nthen to satisfy $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nthe smallest value of $N$ we can pick is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{N}} ={\\color{blue}{20}}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate a few terms of the sequence to understand concretely what this result means:\n\n- We expect that for $a_n$ is larger than $\\varepsilon = 0.000\\, 4$ when $n={\\color{red}{19}}$. Indeed,\n$$\n\\displaystyle a_{\\color{red}{19}} = \\left(\\dfrac23\\right)^{\\color{red}{19}} \\approx 0.000\\,451 > \\varepsilon.\n$$\n- However, *for all* $n\\geq {\\color{blue}{N}} ={\\color{blue}{20}}$, we have that $\\mid a_n \\mid$ is smaller than $\\varepsilon = 0.000\\,4{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{{\\color{blue}{20}}} & ={\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{{\\color{blue}{20}}}\\approx 0.000\\hspace{0.167em}301<ε\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ {a}_{21} & ={\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{21}\\approx 0.000\\hspace{0.167em}200<ε\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ {a}_{22} & ={\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{22}\\approx 0.000\\hspace{0.167em}134<ε\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ {a}_{{\\color{purple}{23}}} & ={\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{{\\color{purple}{23}}}\\approx 0.000\\hspace{0.167em}089<ε\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe smallest value of $N$ such that $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nentirely depends on which value of $\\varepsilon$ we select.\n\nFor example, if we selected\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon = 0.000\\,1\n$$\n\nthen we'd need a larger value of $N$ to satisfy $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nWe can see from the above calculations that we would select\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{N}}={\\color{purple}{23}}\n$$\n\nIndeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{22} &= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{22} \\approx 0.000134 > ε\\cdot \\\\\na_{23} &= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{23} \\approx 0.000089 < ε✓ \\\\\na_{24} &= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{24} \\approx 0.000059 < ε✓ \\\\\na_{24} &= \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)^{25} \\approx 0.000040 < ε✓ \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the defining feature of a null sequence is that *no matter how small* $\\varepsilon$ is, we can *always* find an $N$ such that $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nFinally, since $N$ always depends on $\\varepsilon$, we should think of $N$ as a *function* of $\\varepsilon$, i.e.,\n\n$$\nN= N(\\varepsilon)\n$$\n\nWe won't always write this explicitly, but you should keep this idea firmly in mind.\n\nProving that a given sequence is null relies on finding $N(\\varepsilon)$ for a given sequence. We'll return to this in future lessons."] [17592186199607 :tutorial/content "Consider the following sequence once more:\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{1}{n^2}, \\qquad n \\geq 1\n$$\n\nLet's now discuss how to construct a formal proof that $a_n$ is null:\n\nFirst, recall that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nmeans (using formal notation) that\n\n$$\n\\forall \\varepsilon > 0, \\: \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\forall n \\in \\mathbb{N}, \\: n \\geq N \\: \\Rightarrow \\: \\left| \\dfrac{1}{n^2} \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof by writing this in words:\n\n> *We need to show that for any real $\\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\geq N$, we have*\n> $\\left| \\dfrac{1}{n^2} \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon$.\n\nEarlier in the lesson, we showed that for any $\\varepsilon > 0$, if we let $N$ be the smallest natural number such that\n\n$$\nN > \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon}}\n$$\n\nthen $\\mid a_n \\mid < \\varepsilon$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nUsing this result, we continue the proof:\n\n> *Suppose $\\varepsilon > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt\\varepsilon}$.*\n\nWith that in mind, we show that $\\varepsilon$ is an upper bound of $\\mid a_n \\mid$ for\n\n$$\nn\\geq N{:}\n$$\n\n> *Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have*\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}| & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & <\\frac{1}{(1/\\sqrt{ε})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1/ε} \\\\  & =ε\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\n> *Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\right) = 0.\n$$*\n\nNow that we've figured out all the details, let's write down our formal proof."] [17592186199608 :tutorial/content "When writing a formal mathematical proof, we usually include only the essential details and exclude any additional commentary.\n\nSo, when communicating the full proof that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\n, we could write our proof as follows:\n\n> *We need to show that for any real $\\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n \\geq N$, we have*\n> $\\left| \\dfrac{1}{n^2} \\right| \\lt \\varepsilon$.\n> *Suppose $\\varepsilon > 0$. Let's choose some natural number $N > \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt\\varepsilon}$.*\n> *Then, for any natural number $n \\geq N$, we have*\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}| & =\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}} \\\\  & <\\frac{1}{(1/\\sqrt{ε})^{2}} \\\\  & =ε\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> *Therefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle \\lim\\limits_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{n^2}\\right) = 0.\n$$*\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The proof contains no details about how a suitable $N$ is found. For simple sequences, this is usually worked out separately and the details are omitted from the formal proof. However, if a sequence is more complicated and finding a suitable $N(\\varepsilon)$ is non-trivial, you might consider including *some* of the details of how $N$ is found in the formal proof.\n- Notice that we skipped a few steps in the algebra when writing the full proof. This is quite normal. Formal proofs usually only include the steps essential to understanding the core argument. Although, this depends on the author and the intended reader. If the intended readers are new to mathematical proofs, keeping more detail is probably a good idea.\n\nLet's see another example."] [17592186199615 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the geometric sequence\n\n$$\na_n = \\left(\\dfrac97\\right)^n, \\qquad n\\geq 1\n$$\n\nNote that this sequence diverges to infinity since it's a positive geometric sequence with common ratio\n\n$$\nr = \\dfrac97 > 1{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac97\\right)^n = \\infty\n$$\n\nEarlier, we showed that if we select\n\n$$\nM = 8\\,000\\,000\n$$\n\nthen to satisfy $a_n > M$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nthe smallest value of $N$ we can pick is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{N}} ={\\color{blue}{64}}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate a few terms of the sequence to understand concretely what this result means:\n\n- We expect that $a_n$ for $n={\\color{red}{63}}$ is smaller than $M = 8\\,000\\,000$. Indeed,\n$$\n\\displaystyle a_{\\color{red}{63}} = \\left(\\dfrac97\\right)^{\\color{red}{63}} \\approx 7\\,518\\,000 < M.\n$$\n- However, *for all* $n\\geq {\\color{blue}{N}} ={\\color{blue}{64}}$, we have that $a_n$ is larger than $M= 8\\,000\\,000{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{{\\color{blue}{64}}} & ={\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)}^{{\\color{blue}{64}}}\\approx 9\\hspace{0.167em}666\\hspace{0.167em}000>M\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ {a}_{65} & ={\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)}^{65}\\approx 12\\hspace{0.167em}428\\hspace{0.167em}000>M\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\ {a}_{{\\color{purple}{66}}} & ={\\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)}^{{\\color{purple}{66}}}\\approx 15\\hspace{0.167em}978\\hspace{0.167em}000>M\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}{✓}} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe smallest value of $N$ such that $a_n > M$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nentirely depends on which value of $M$ we select.\n\nFor example, if we selected\n\n$$\nM = 15\\,000\\,000\n$$\n\nthen we'd need a larger value of $N$ to satisfy $a_n > M$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nWe can see from the above calculations that we would select\n\n$$\n{\\color{purple}{N}}={\\color{purple}{66}}\n$$\n\nIndeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{65} &= \\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)^{65} \\approx 12428000 < M\\cdot \\\\\na_{66} &= \\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)^{66} \\approx 15978000 > M✓ \\\\\na_{67} &= \\left(\\frac{9}{7}\\right)^{67} \\approx 20544000 > M✓ \\\\\n&= ⋮\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the defining feature of a sequence with a (positive) infinite limit is that *no matter how large* $M$ is, we can *always* find an $N$ such that $a_n > M$ for all\n\n$$\nn\\geq N\n$$\n\nFinally, since $N$ always depends on $M$, we should think of $N$ as a *function* of $M$, i.e.,\n$N= N(M)$.\nWe won't always write this explicitly, but you should keep this idea firmly in mind.\n\nProving that a given sequence diverges to infinity relies on finding $N(M)$ for a given sequence. We'll return to this in future lessons."] [17592186199620 :tutorial/content "We wish to prove the following statement:\n\n$$\nm \\text{is even and} n \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m + 2n \\text{is even}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the proof step-by-step. We start by assuming that the antecedent is true:\n\n> **Step 1:** *Let $m$ and $n$ be integers, where $m$ is even and $n$ is odd.*\n\nNow, we use the definitions of evenness and oddness.\n\n> **Step 2:** *Then, $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$ for some integers $a$ and $b$.*\n\n**Watch Out**! We must use two *different* integers to represent the evenness of $m$ and the oddness of $n$. In this case, we use $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to substitute $m=2a$ and\n\n$$\nn=2b+1\n$$\n\ninto our expression $m + 2n$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2$.\n\n> **Step 3:** *Substituting $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$ into the expression, we get*\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}m+2n & =(2a)+2(2b+1) \\\\  & =2a+4b+2 \\\\  & =2\\underbrace{(a+2b+1)}_{c}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer\n\n$$\nc=a + 2b + 1\n$$\n\n(highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\nm + 2n = 2c\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $m + 2n$ is indeed a multiple of $2$.\n\n> *Let $c=a + 2b + 1$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, $c$ is an integer. As a result, we have*\n> $\\begin{aligned}m+2n & =2\\underbrace{(a+2b+1)}_{c} \\\\  & =2c\\end{aligned}$\n> *which is a multiple of $2$.*\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion:\n\n> **Step 4:** *Therefore, $m + 2n$ is even.*\n\nNow that we've figured out all the details, let's write down the full proof."] [17592186199621 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n\n$$\nm \\text{is even and} n \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m + 2n \\text{is even}\n$$\n\nProof:\n\n> *Let $m$ and $n$ be integers, where $m$ is even and $n$ is odd.*\n> *Then, $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$ for some integers $a$ and $b$.*\n> *Substituting $m=2a$ and $n=2b+1$ into the expression, we get*\n> $\\begin{aligned}m+2n & =2a+4b+2 \\\\  & =2(a+2b+1) \\\\  & =2c\\end{aligned}$\n> *which is a multiple of $2$.*\n> *Therefore, $m + 2n$ is even.*"] [17592186199622 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n\n$$\nnis an integer⇒n^{2} - 3nis even\n$$\n\nProof:\n\n> *Let $n$ be an integer. We consider the following cases:*\n> ***Case 1.*** *If $n$ is even, then $n = 2a$ for some integer $a$.*\n> *As a result, we obtain*\n> $\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-3n & =(2a)^{2}-3(2a) \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}-6a \\\\  & =2(2{a}^{2}-3a).\\end{aligned}$.\n> *Let $b = 2a^2 - 3a$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.*\n> *So, $n^2-3n = 2b$, which means that it is even.*\n> ***Case 2.*** *If $n$ is odd, then $n = 2a+1$ for some integer $a$.*\n> *As a result, we obtain*\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-3n & =(2a+1)^{2}-3(2a+1) \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}-2a-2 \\\\  & =2(2{a}^{2}-a-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> *Let $b = 2a^2 - a - 1$. Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.*\n> *So, $n^2-3n = 2b$, which means it is even.*\n> *Combining the cases above, we can conclude that $n^2-3n$ is even for any integer $n$.*"] [17592186199623 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n\n$$\nmn \\text{is even} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad m \\text{is even or} n \\text{is even}\n$$\n\nProof:\n\n> *The contrapositive form of the given statement is as follows:*\n> $m \\text{is odd and} n \\text{is odd} \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad mn \\text{is odd}$\n> *If $m$ and $n$ are odd, then*\n> $m = 2a+1 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad n = 2b+1$,\n> *where $a$ and $b$ are integers.*\n> *Therefore,*\n> $\\begin{aligned}mn & =(2a+1)(2b+1) \\\\  & =4ab+2a+2b+1 \\\\  & =2(2ab+a+b)+1.\\end{aligned}$.\n> *Let $c=2ab+a+b$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.*\n> *Hence, we have*\n> $\\begin{aligned}mn & =2(2ab+a+b)+1 \\\\  & =2c+1,\\end{aligned}$,\n> *which is one larger than a multiple of $2$.*\n> *Therefore, $mn$ is odd.*"] [17592186203665 :question/worked-solution "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $J_1$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{KL}$ | Given |\n| 2 | $KJ_1 = \\dfrac12 KL$ | Definition of a midpoint |\n| 3 | $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{KJ_1}{KL} = \\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$ | Multiplication principle |\n| 4 | $L_1$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{JK}$ | Given |\n| 5 | $KL_1 = \\dfrac12 JK$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of a midpoint}}$ |\n| 6 | $\\dfrac{KL_1}{JK} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ | Multiplication principle |\n| 7 | $\\dfrac{KL_1}{KJ} = \\dfrac{KJ_1}{KL}$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 8 | $\\angle{JKL} \\cong \\angle{L_1KJ_1}$ | Reflexive property of congruence |\n| 9 | $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\triangle{JKL} \\sim \\triangle{L_1KJ_1}}$ | SAS similarity criterion |\n| 10 | $\\dfrac{J_1L_1}{JL} = \\dfrac{KJ_1}{KL}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{CPSTP}}$ |\n| 11 | $\\dfrac{J_1L_1}{JL} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 12 | $J_1L_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2} JL$ | Multiplication principle |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 3. By the multiplication principle, we can divide the equation we obtained in row 2 by $KL$, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K{J}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}KL \\\\ \\frac{K{J}_{1}}{KL} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{KL}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{KL}} \\\\ \\frac{K{J}_{1}}{KL} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider row 5. From row 4, we have that $L_1$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{JK}$. Therefore, by the definition of a midpoint,\n$KL_1 = \\dfrac12 JK$.\n- Consider row 9. From rows 7 and 8, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{KL_1}{KJ} = \\dfrac{KJ_1}{KL}, \\qquad \\angle{JKL} \\cong \\angle{L_1KJ_1}.\n$$\nTherefore, by the SAS similarity criterion, $\\triangle{JKL} \\sim \\triangle{L_1KJ_1}$.\n- Consider row 10. From row 9, we have that triangles $\\triangle{JKL}$ and $\\triangle{L_1KJ_1}$ are similar. Corresponding parts of similar triangles are in proportion (CPSTP). Therefore, we have\n$\\dfrac{J_1L_1}{JL} = \\dfrac{KJ_1}{KL}$."] [17592186203672 :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n> $P: \\quad \\sqrt[3]{5}$ is irrational\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n> $\\lnot P: \\quad \\sqrt[3]{5}$ is rational\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ is rational.\n\nIf a number is rational, it can be written as a fraction in its lowest terms, i.e., where the numerator and denominator are coprime.\n\n> Then,\n> $\\sqrt[3]{5} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$\n> for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$, where $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.\n\nThe idea is to cube both sides of the equation to find a factor of $a$ and a factor of $b$.\n\n> This implies that\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{5} & =\\frac{a}{b} \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{5})^{3} & ={\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)}^{3} \\\\ 5 & =\\frac{{a}^{3}}{{b}^{3}} \\\\ 5{b}^{3} & ={a}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> which means that $5 \\mid a^3$.\n\nNow, recall the following property of divisibility by a prime $p{:}$\n$p \\mid xyz \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (p \\mid x) \\lor (p \\mid y) \\lor (p \\mid z)$\nApplying this property with $p = 5$ and\n\n$$\nx = y = z = a\n$$\n\nwe have\n$5 \\mid a^3 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad 5 \\mid a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (5 \\mid a) \\lor (5 \\mid a) \\lor (5 \\mid a)$.\nSo, we have that $5 \\mid a$.\n\n> Since $5$ is prime, we have $5 \\mid a$, which means $a = 5c$ where $c$ is an integer.\n\nNow, we substitute $a = 5c$ into the equation $5b^3 = a^3$ and show that $5$ is also a factor of $b$.\n\n> Therefore, we have that\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}5{b}^{3} & =(5c)^{3} \\\\ 5{b}^{3} & =125{c}^{3} \\\\ {b}^{3} & =25{c}^{3} \\\\ {b}^{3} & =5\\cdot 5{c}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> Hence, by the definition of divisibility, $5 \\mid b^3$, which means that $5 \\mid b$.\n\nFinally, since $a$ and $b$ have a common factor of $5$, their greatest common divisor is at least $5$. This gives us our contradiction.\n\n> Combining the results $5 \\mid a$ and $5 \\mid b$, we conclude that $\\gcd(a,b) \\geq 5$. But this contradicts the assumption that $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $\\sqrt[3]{5}$ is irrational."] [17592186203675 :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP{:} \\quad 3-\\sqrt{2} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad 3-\\sqrt{2} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.\n\nSince we're assuming $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is rational, we can express it as the ratio of two non-zero integers:\n\n> Then $3-\\sqrt{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to rearrange the equation, leaving a root on one side and a fraction on the other.\n\n> So, we have\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{a}{b} \\\\ -\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{a}{b}-3 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =3-\\frac{a}{b} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{3b}{b}-\\frac{a}{b} \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\overbrace{\\frac{3b-a}{b}}^{c}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define $c=3b-a$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}=\\dfrac{c}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.\n\n> Let $c=3b-a$. Since $a$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\sqrt{2} = \\frac{c}{b}$,\n> which is rational.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $3-\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational."] [17592186203693 :question/problem "Consider the relation $R$ defined over $X=\\{a, b, c, d, e\\}$ and $Y=\\{0,1\\}$, and the relation $S$ defined over $Y=\\{0,1\\}$ and $Z=\\{4, 7, 10\\}$, given below.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =\\{(a,0),(a,1),(c,0),(d,1),(e,0),(e,1)\\} \\\\ S & =\\{(1,4),(1,7),(1,10)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nExpress the composition $S \\circ R$ as a list of ordered pairs."] [17592186203714 :question/problem "Multiply the following integers in $\\mathbb Z_{10}$. Use the smallest nonnegative representative for the resulting class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\hspace{0.167em}2\\hspace{0.167em}]\\cdot [\\hspace{0.167em}4\\hspace{0.167em}] & =[\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}{?}}\\hspace{0.167em}] \\\\ [\\hspace{0.167em}4\\hspace{0.167em}]\\cdot [\\hspace{0.167em}9\\hspace{0.167em}] & =[\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}{?}}\\hspace{0.167em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186203715 :question/problem "Perform the following operations in $\\mathbb Z_{11}$. Use the smallest nonnegative representative for the resulting classes.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\hspace{0.167em}9\\hspace{0.167em}]+[\\hspace{0.167em}5\\hspace{0.167em}] & =[\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}{?}}\\hspace{0.167em}] \\\\ [\\hspace{0.167em}6\\hspace{0.167em}]-[\\hspace{0.167em}10\\hspace{0.167em}] & =[\\hspace{0.167em}{\\color{blue}{?}}\\hspace{0.167em}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186203723 :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n> $P: \\quad \\sqrt[3]{15}$ is irrational\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n> $\\lnot P: \\quad \\sqrt[3]{15}$ is rational\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\sqrt[3]{15}$ is rational.\n\nIf a number is rational, it can be written as a fraction in its lowest terms, i.e., where the numerator and denominator are coprime.\n\n> Then,\n> $\\sqrt[3]{15} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$\n> for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$, where $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.\n\nThe idea is to cube both sides of the equation to find a factor of $a$ and a factor of $b$.\n\n> This implies that\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{15} & =\\frac{a}{b} \\\\ {(\\sqrt[3]{15})}^{3} & ={\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)}^{3} \\\\ 15 & =\\frac{{a}^{3}}{{b}^{3}} \\\\ 15{b}^{3} & ={a}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> which means that $15 \\mid a^3$.\n\nWe need to show that $15\\mid a$.\n\n> Let's use this to show that $15\\mid a$.\n\nTo do this, recall the following divisibility properties:\n\n- Transitivity of divisibility:\n$(x\\mid y) \\land (y\\mid z) \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad x\\mid z$\n- Divisibility of a power by a prime:\n$p \\mid a^n \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad p \\mid a$\n- Divisibility by a product:\n$(x\\mid z)\\land(y\\mid z)\\land(\\textrm{gcd}(x,y) = 1) \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad xy\\mid z$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\n15=3\\cdot 5\n$$\n\nis the product of two primes, which suggests we may want to use the third property.\n\nAlso, if $15$ divides $a^3$, then $3$ and $5$ must divide $a^3$ by the first property.\n\nWe start by using the first property:\n\n> First, note that $15 = 3\\cdot 5$. By the transitivity of divisibility,\n> $(3∣15)∧(15∣a^{3})⇒3∣a^{3}$.\n\nNow, we use the second property:\n\n> Since $3$ is prime, this means that $3 \\mid a$.\n\nThe argument to show that $5\\mid a$ is identical, so we simply state that.\n\n> We also have $5 \\mid a$ by a similar argument.\n\nSince $3$ and $5$ are coprime, we can show that $15\\mid a$ by the third property.\n\n> Since $3\\mid a$ and $5\\mid a$, and $\\textrm{gcd(3,5)} =1$, we have that $15\\mid a$ which means that $a=15c$ for some integer $c$.\n\nNow, we substitute $a=15c$ into the equation $15b^3 = a^3$ and show that $15$ is also a factor of $b$.\n\n> Therefore, we have that\n> $\\begin{aligned}15{b}^{3} & =(15c)^{3} \\\\ 15{b}^{3} & =3,375{c}^{3} \\\\ {b}^{3} & =225{c}^{3}.\\end{aligned}$.\n\nThen, we use the first property again:\n\n> Hence, by the definition of divisibility, $225 \\mid b^3$. By the transitivity of divisibility,\n> $(15∣225)∧(225∣b^{3})⇒15∣b^{3}$,\n> which means that $15 \\mid b$.\n\nFinally, since $a$ and $b$ have a common factor of $15$, their greatest common divisor is at least $15$. This gives us our contradiction.\n\n> Combining the results $15 \\mid a$ and $15 \\mid b$, we conclude that $\\gcd(a,b) \\geq 15$. But this contradicts the assumption that $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $\\sqrt[3]{15}$ is irrational."] [17592186203724 :question/worked-solution "Let's write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\nP{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is rational.\n\nSince we're assuming\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n$$\n\nis rational, we can express it as the ratio of two non-zero integers:\n\n> Then $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the equation to give a radical on one side and a fraction on the other.\n\n> So, we have\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{a}{b} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{\\overbrace{2a}^{c}}{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define the integer $c=2a$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}=\\dfrac{c}{b}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.\n\n> Let $c=2a$. Since $2$ and $a$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.\n> Therefore, we have\n> $\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{c}{b}$,\n> which is rational.\n> But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> Therefore, $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is irrational."] [17592186203726 :question/worked-solution "To show that $A\\subseteq B$, we need to show that every element of $A$ is also an element of $B$.\n\nSo, we begin our proof as follows.\n\n> To prove that $A \\subseteq B$, we take an arbitrary element of $A$ and show that its is an element of $B$.\n\nNow, we denote an arbitrary element of $A$ by $x$ and use the definition of $A$.\n\n> So, let $x \\in A$. Then, according to the definition of $A$, there exists $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that\n> $x = 9^{m+2}$.\n\nThe key to unlocking the proof is to rewrite $x$ to match the definition of $B$. In other words, we need to write $x=3^n$, where $n$ is an integer.\n\n> Note that\n> $\\begin{aligned}x & ={9}^{m+2} \\\\  & ={({3}^{2})}^{m+2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2(m+2)}.\\end{aligned}$.\n> Denote $n=2(m+2)$. Since $m$ is an integer, $n$ is also an integer. Thus,\n> $x=3^n$,\n> where $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. So, by the definition of $B$, we have $x \\in B$.\n\nSo, we have shown that $x\\in B$, and we can state our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $A \\subseteq B$."] [17592186203728 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $A = B$ if and only if $A\\subseteq B$ and $B\\subseteq A$.\n\nSo, we begin our proof as follows:\n\n> To prove that $A=B$, we must show that $A \\subseteq B$ and $B \\subseteq A$.\n\nFirst, let's prove that $A \\subseteq B$. We denote an arbitrary element of $A$ by $x$ and use the definition of $A$.\n\n> **Part 1.** Proving that $A \\subseteq B$.\n> Let $x \\in A$. Then, there exists $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that $x = 3m+1$.\n\nNext, we rewrite $x$ to match the definition of $B$. In other words, we need to write\n\n$$\nx=3n+7\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n> Note that\n> $\\begin{aligned}x & =3m+1 \\\\  & =3m+(7-6) \\\\  & =3m-6+7 \\\\  & =3(m-2)+7.\\end{aligned}$.\n> Denoting $n=m-2$, we get that\n> $x=3n+7$,\n> where $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. So, by the definition of $B$, we have $x \\in B$. Thus, $A \\subseteq B$.\n\nNext, let's prove that $B \\subseteq A$.\n\n> **Part 2.** Proving that $B \\subseteq A$.\n> Let $x \\in B$. Then, there exists $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that $x = 3n+7$.\n\nNext, we rewrite $x$ to match the definition of $A$. In other words, we need to write\n\n$$\nx=3m+1\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is an integer.\n\n> Note that\n> $\\begin{aligned}x & =3n+7 \\\\  & =3n+(6+1) \\\\  & =3(n+2)+1.\\end{aligned}$.\n> Denoting $m=n+2$, we get that\n> $x=3m+1$,\n> where $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. So, by the definition of $A$, we have $x \\in A$. Thus, $B \\subseteq A$.\n\nFinally, we've shown that $A\\subseteq B$ and $B\\subseteq A$. Therefore, $A=B$, and we state our conclusion.\n\n> Therefore, $A = B$."] [17592186203730 :question/worked-solution "The connector \"if and only if\" means we must prove an equivalence.\n\n$$\nP \\Leftrightarrow Q\n$$\n\nHowever, instead of proving an equivalence directly, we can instead prove two conditional statements.\n\n$$\nP\\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv (P\\Rightarrow Q) \\land (Q\\Rightarrow P)\n$$\n\nSo, we start our proof as follows:\n\nWe need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n(x+y)^2 =x^2+y^2 \\quad \\Leftrightarrow \\quad \\big(x=0 \\quad \\lor \\quad y=0 \\big)\n$$\n\nThis is the same as proving two implications.\n\nFirst, we consider the \"left to right\" implication.\n\n**Case $\\Rightarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n\n$$\n(x+y)^2 =x^2+y^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\big(x=0 \\quad \\lor \\quad y=0 \\big)\n$$\n\nBy expanding the left-hand side and simplifying, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 2xy + y^{2} &= x^{2} + y^{2} \\\\\n2xy &= 0 \\\\\nx \\cdot y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the left-hand side equals zero if $x=0$ or $y=0$.\n\nNext, we consider the \"right to left\" implication.\n\n> **Case $\\Leftarrow{:}$** We need to show that\n> $$\n\\displaystyle \\big(x=0 \\quad \\lor \\quad y=0 \\big) \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad (x+y)^2= x^2+y^2.\n$$\n\nHere, consider the two cases separately.\n\n> We have two subcases:\n> **Subcase 1.** Let $x=0$. Then, substituting this into our equation, we obtain\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (0+y)^{2} & ={0}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={y}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> which is always true.\n\nAnd the second case.\n\n> **Subcase 2.** Let $y=0$. Then, substituting this into our equation, we obtain\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ (x+0)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{0}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={x}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> which is always true.\n> Hence, if $x=0$ or $y=0$ then $(x+y)^2=x^2+y^2$.\n\nFinally, we write down the conclusion:\n\n> Therefore, $(x+y)^2=x^2+y^2$ if and only if $x=0$ or $y=0$."] [17592186203731 :question/worked-solution "To show that $A\\subseteq B$, we need to show that every element of $A$ is also an element of $B$.\n\nSo, we begin our proof as follows.\n\n> To prove that $A \\subseteq B$, we take an arbitrary element of $A$ and show that its is an element of $B$.\n\nNow, we denote an arbitrary element of $A$ by $x$ and use the definition of $A$.\n\n> So, let $x \\in A$. Then, according to the definition of $A$, there exists $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that\n> $\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =4m \\\\ x & =3+4m.\\end{aligned}$.\n\nThe key to unlocking the proof is to rewrite $x$ to match the definition of $B$. In other words, we need to write\n\n$$\nx = 1+2n\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\n> Note that\n> $\\begin{aligned}x & =3+4m \\\\  & =1+2+4m \\\\  & =1+2(1+2m)\\end{aligned}$\n> Denote $n=1+2m$. Since $m$ is an integer, $n$ is also an integer. Thus,\n> $x=1+2n\\qquad\\Leftrightarrow \\qquad x\\equiv 1\\:(\\textrm{mod} 2)$.\n> Therefore, by the definition of $B$, we have $x \\in B$.\n\nSo, we have shown that $x\\in B$, and we can state our conclusion.\n\n> So, we conclude that $A \\subseteq B$."] [17592186203761 :question/worked-solution "The diagram corresponding to our automaton is shown below.\n\n![](images/0c/0c79f07e5e39ef11f5366fadd2ad37478a925c2296b498f647553c099e4572de.png)\n\nLet's apply the extended transition function to our strings.\n\n- Consider the string $0001{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (p,0001) & =\\delta (\\delta (p,0),001) \\\\  & =\\delta (q,001) \\\\  & =\\delta (\\delta (q,0),01) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,01) \\\\  & =\\delta (\\delta (r,0),1) \\\\  & =\\delta (p,1) \\\\  & =p\\end{aligned}\n$$![](images/5b/5bdbfd194560c41eb304d9bf489623ab0f82e1bb28b24cac9573f599afe017b2.png)\nThe process is terminated at the state $p$. Since $p$ is not a final (accepting) state, the string $0001$ is rejected.\n- Consider the string $1010{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (p,1010) & =\\delta (\\delta (p,1),010) \\\\  & =\\delta (p,010) \\\\  & =\\delta (\\delta (p,0),10) \\\\  & =\\delta (q,10) \\\\  & =\\delta (\\delta (q,1),0) \\\\  & =\\delta (q,0) \\\\  & =r\\end{aligned}\n$$![](images/7d/7d75e94dd273fc37034baefc52234aad4056f528ec5824ddf850e729d44931c0.png)\nThe process is terminated at the state $r$. Since $r$ is a final (accepting) state, the string $1010$ is accepted.\n- Consider the string $0011{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\delta (p,0011) & =\\delta (\\delta (p,0),011) \\\\  & =\\delta (q,011) \\\\  & =\\delta (\\delta (q,0),11) \\\\  & =\\delta (r,11) \\\\  & =\\delta (\\delta (r,1),1) \\\\  & =\\delta (q,1) \\\\  & =q\\end{aligned}\n$$![](images/a4/a497ef18d08c2cbefcf7dc7164d3073f2b64851c1124fb4f22e573804570d6d2.png)\nThe process is terminated at the state $q$. Since $q$ is a final (accepting) state, the string $0011$ is accepted.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186203766 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the one-factor ANOVA table for comparing $K$ groups in a sample of size $n$ gives the following data.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Source</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{SS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,df\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{MS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,F\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Between Groups</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{SSB}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$K-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{MSB}=\\dfrac{\\text{SSB}}{K-1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Within Groups</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{SSW}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$n-K$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{MSW}=\\dfrac{\\text{SSW}}{n-K}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Total</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{SST}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$n-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\text{SSW}$ is the sum of squares within groups,\n- $\\text{SSB}$ is the sum of squares between groups,\n- $\\text{SST}$ is the total sum of squares, and $\\text{SST}=\\text{SSW}+\\text{SSB}$.\n\nLet's go through the missing entries in the given table.\n\n- $\\text{SSB}$ can be computed using the above relation:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SSB} & =\\text{SST}-\\text{SSW} \\\\  & =7.6-4.15 \\\\  & =3.45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The degrees of freedom of $\\text{SSB}$ are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d{f}_{B} & =K-1 \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The degrees of freedom of $\\text{SSW}$ are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d{f}_{W} & =n-K \\\\  & =26-3 \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The mean sum of squares between groups is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{MSB} & =\\frac{\\text{SSB}}{K-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.45}{3-1} \\\\  & =1.725 \\\\  & \\approx 1.73.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The mean sum of squares within groups is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{MSW} & =\\frac{\\text{SSW}}{n-K} \\\\  & =\\frac{4.15}{26-3} \\\\  & =0.180\\hspace{0.167em}434… \\\\  & \\approx 0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Finally, the $F$ ratio is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}} = \\dfrac{1.725}{0.180\\,434\\ldots} \\approx 9.56.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as follows.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Source</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{SS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,df\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{MS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,F\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Between</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}3.45}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.73}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}9.56}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Within</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4.15$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.18}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Total</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$7.6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$25$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186203775 :question/worked-solution "![](images/de/def8939d5b6a390496bbf83b2b5d595980c27189c4cbfea3af1e492dba4688b9.png)\n\nA sketch of the given information is shown above, and the correct proof is below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$r \\perp s$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$m\\angle a= 90^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of perpendicular lines</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$m\\angle a+ m\\angle b=180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of supplementary angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$90^\\circ + m\\angle b = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Substitution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$m\\angle b = 90^\\circ$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$90^\\circ = m\\angle b$</td>\n<td>Symmetric property of equality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$m\\angle a = m\\angle b$</td>\n<td>Transitive property of equality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$\\angle a \\cong \\angle b$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 3. Notice that $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ form a linear pair. Therefore, by the linear pair postulate, these angles are supplementary.\n- Consider row 6. From row 5, we have that $90^\\circ + m\\angle b = 180^\\circ$. By the addition principle, we can subtract $90^\\circ$ from both sides of this equation:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{90}^{∘}+m\\mathrm{∠}b & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {90}^{∘}+m\\mathrm{∠}b-{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘}-{90}^{∘} \\\\ \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{90}^{∘}}+m\\mathrm{∠}b-\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{90}^{∘}} & ={90}^{∘} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}b & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider row 9. From row 8, $\\angle a$ and $\\angle b$ have the same measures. Therefore, by definition of congruent angles, these angles are congruent."] [17592186203778 :question/worked-solution "The correct proof is below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$\\angle DCB$ and $\\angle DCA$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of supplementary angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$m\\angle DCA =180^\\circ - m\\angle DCB$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$m\\angle DCA = 90^\\circ$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Substitution}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$m\\angle DCB = m\\angle DCA$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$\\angle DCB \\cong \\angle DCA$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of perpendicular bisector}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$\\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">10</td>\n<td>$\\triangle{DCB} \\cong \\triangle{DCA}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{SAS criterion}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">11</td>\n<td>$\\overline{DB} \\cong \\overline{DA}$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{CPCTC}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n1. We're given that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$. Then, by the definition of perpendicular lines, the angles formed by the intersection of $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ and $\\overline{AB}$ have a measure of $90^\\circ$. So, we have that $m\\angle{DCB} = 90^\\circ$.\n2. Notice that $\\angle{DCB}$ and $\\angle{DCA}$ form a linear pair. Therefore, these angles are supplementary by the linear pair postulate.\n3. From row 2, $\\angle{DCB}$ and $\\angle{DCA}$ are supplementary. Therefore, $m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$ by the definition of supplementary angles.\n4. From row 3, $m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$. By the addition principle, we can subtract $m\\angle{DCB}$ from both sides of this equation:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}DCB+m\\mathrm{∠}DCA & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}DCB+m\\mathrm{∠}DCA-m\\mathrm{∠}DCB & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}DCB \\\\ \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{m\\mathrm{∠}DCB}+m\\mathrm{∠}DCA-\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{m\\mathrm{∠}DCB} & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}DCB \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}DCA & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}DCB\\end{aligned}\n$$\n5. From rows 1 and 4, $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$ and $m\\angle DCA =180^\\circ - m\\angle DCB$. Therefore, by substituting the first into the second,\n$$\n\\displaystyle m\\angle DCA\n\n= 180^\\circ - 90^\\circ\n= 90^\\circ.\n$$\n6. From rows 1 and 5, we have $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ = m\\angle DCA$. Therefore, by the transitive property of equality,\n$m\\angle{DCB} = m\\angle{DCA}$.\n7. From row 6, $\\angle{DCB}$ and $m\\angle{DCA}$ have the same measure. Therefore, these angles are congruent by the definition of congruent angles.\n8. From the given information, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$ and $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}$. By the definition of a perpendicular bisector, we have\n$\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}$.\n9. The reflexive property of congruence states that every segment is congruent to itself. Then, $\\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$ by the reflexive property of congruence.\n10. From rows 8, 7, and 9, we have\n$\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}, \\qquad \\angle DCB \\cong \\angle DCA, \\qquad \\overline{CD} \\cong \\overline{CD}$.\nTherefore, by the SAS criterion, $\\triangle{DCB} \\cong \\triangle{DCA}$.\n11. From row 10, $\\triangle{{\\color{red}D}C{\\color{red}B}} \\cong \\triangle{{\\color{red}D}C{\\color{red}A}}$. Then, $\\overline{DB} \\cong \\overline{DA}$ by CPCTC."] [17592186203781 :question/worked-solution "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-border-top=\"none\" data-border-left=\"none\"></th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Statement</th>\n<th data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Reason</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">1</td>\n<td>$\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">2</td>\n<td>$m\\angle CHF + m\\angle FHD = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of supplementary angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">3</td>\n<td>$m\\angle CHF = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">4</td>\n<td>$\\angle EGB$ and $\\angle BGH$ are supplementary</td>\n<td>Linear pair postulate</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">5</td>\n<td>$m\\angle EGB + m\\angle BGH = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Definition of supplementary angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">6</td>\n<td>$\\angle EGB \\cong \\angle CHF$</td>\n<td>Given</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">7</td>\n<td>$m\\angle EGB = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">8</td>\n<td>$(180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD) + m\\angle BGH = 180^\\circ$</td>\n<td>Substitution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">9</td>\n<td>$m\\angle BGH = m\\angle FHD$</td>\n<td>Addition principle</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">10</td>\n<td>$\\angle BGH \\cong \\angle FHD$</td>\n<td>Definition of congruent angles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">11</td>\n<td>$\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ are parallel</td>\n<td>$\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Converse corresponding angles postulate}}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 1. Notice from the diagram that $\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ form a linear pair. The linear pair postulate states that such angles must be supplementary. Therefore, $\\angle CHF$ and $\\angle FHD$ are supplementary angles.\n- Consider row 3. From row 2, we have $m\\angle CHF + m\\angle FHD = 180^\\circ$. By the addition principle, we can subtract $m\\angle FHD$ from both sides\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}CHF+m\\mathrm{∠}FHD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}CHF+m\\mathrm{∠}FHD-m\\mathrm{∠}FHD & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}FHD \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}CHF+\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{m\\mathrm{∠}FHD}-\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{m\\mathrm{∠}FHD} & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}FHD \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}CHF & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}FHD.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider row 7. From row 3, we have that $m\\angle CHF = 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$, and from row 6, we have that $\\angle EGB \\cong \\angle CHF$. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, these angles have equal measure:\n$m \\angle EGB= 180^\\circ - m\\angle FHD$\n- Consider row 11. From row 10, we have that $\\angle BGH\\cong \\angle FHD$. By the converse corresponding angles postulate, if two lines and a transversal form corresponding angles that are congruent, then the lines are parallel. Notice that $\\angle BGH$ and $\\angle FHD$ are corresponding angles formed by the lines $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ with the transversal $\\overleftrightarrow{EF}$. Therefore, $\\overleftrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overleftrightarrow{CD}$ are parallel."] [17592186203789 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $J$ in terms of the coordinates of $S$ and $T{:}$\n\n> Since $\\overline{RJ}$ is a median, we have that $J$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{ST}$. So, if $(x_J,y_J)$ are the coordinates of $J$, by the\n> midpoint formula, we have\n> $$\n\\displaystyle x_{J} = \\frac{x_{S} + x_{T}}{2}, y_{J} = \\frac{y_{S} + y_{T}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we write the coordinates of the centroid using the formula for partitioning a line segment in a given ratio:\n\n> By the centroid ratio theorem, the point $G$ splits the median $\\overline{RJ}$ in the ratio $2:1$, starting from the point $R$. Now, by partitioning the segment $\\overline{RJ}$ in a given ratio, we obtain that\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{G} & =\\frac{{x}_{R}+2{x}_{J}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{R}+2\\cdot \\frac{{x}_{S}+{x}_{T}}{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{R}+{x}_{S}+{x}_{T}}{3}, \\\\ {y}_{G} & =\\frac{{y}_{R}+2{y}_{J}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}_{R}+2\\cdot \\frac{{y}_{S}+{y}_{T}}{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}_{R}+{y}_{S}+{y}_{T}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186203791 :question/worked-solution "The correct proof is shown below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $\\dfrac{PA}{PR} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ | Given |\n| 2 | $B$ is the midpoint of $\\overline{PQ}$ | Given |\n| 3 | $PB = \\dfrac12{PQ}$ | Definition of a midpoint |\n| 4 | $\\dfrac{PB}{PQ} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Multiplication principle}}$ |\n| 5 | $$\n\\displaystyle \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{PA}{PR} = \\dfrac{PB}{PQ}}\n$$ | Transitive property of equality |\n| 6 | $\\angle{RPQ} \\cong \\angle{APB}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Reflexive property of congruence}}$ |\n| 7 | $\\triangle{RQP} \\sim \\triangle{ABP}$ | SAS similarity criterion |\n| 8 | $\\angle PBA \\cong \\angle Q$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Corresponding angles in similar triangles}}$ |\n| 9 | $\\overline{BA} \\parallel \\overline{RQ}$ | Converse corresponding angles postulate |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 4. By the multiplication principle, we can divide the equation we obtained in row 3 by $PQ$, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PB & =\\frac{1}{2}PQ \\\\ \\frac{PB}{PQ} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{PQ}{PQ} \\\\ \\frac{PB}{PQ} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{PQ}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{PQ}} \\\\ \\frac{PB}{PQ} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider row 5. From rows 1 and 4, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PA}{PR}}} = \\dfrac 12 \\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad \\dfrac 12 = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PB}{PQ}}}.\n$$\nTherefore, by the transitive property of equality, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PA}{PR}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{PB}{PQ}}}.\n$$\n- Consider row 6. Notice that $\\angle RPQ$ and $\\angle APB$ represent the same angle. Therefore, by the reflexive property of congruence, we have $\\angle{RPQ} \\cong \\angle{APB}$.\n- Consider row 8. From row 7, we have\n$\\triangle{RQP} \\sim \\triangle{ABP}$.\nTherefore, we have that $\\angle PBA \\cong \\angle Q$ since they're corresponding angles in similar triangles."] [17592186203816 :question/worked-solution "We have a sample with the following information:\n\n- The sample size is $n = 100$\n- The number of groups is $K = 8$\n\nLet's calculate the degrees of freedom for each of the sums of squares:\n\n- For the total sum of squares $(\\textrm{SST})$, the number of degrees of freedom is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d{f}_{T} & =n-1 \\\\  & =100-1 \\\\  & ={\\color{blue}{99}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the total sum of squares between the groups $(\\textrm{SSB})$, the number of degrees of freedom is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d{f}_{B} & =K-1 \\\\  & =8-1 \\\\  & ={\\color{blue}{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the total sum of squares within the groups $(\\textrm{SSW})$, the number of degrees of freedom is given by\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d{f}_{W} & =n-K \\\\  & =100-8 \\\\  & ={\\color{blue}{92}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\ndf_T = df_B + df_W\n$$"] [17592186203817 :question/worked-solution "The sum of squares within groups $(\\textrm{SSW})$ and sum of squares between groups $(\\textrm{SSB})$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SSW} = \\sum_{k=1}^K \\sum_{j=1}^{n_k} \\left(x_{jk} - \\overline{x}_k\\right)^2, \\qquad \\text{SSB} = \\sum_{k=1}^K n_k \\left(\\overline{x}_k - \\overline{x}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $K$ is the number of groups,\n- $n_k$ is the number of individuals in the $k$th group,\n- $x_{jk}$ is the $j$th data point from the $k$th group,\n- $\\overline{x}_{k}$ is the sample mean of the $k$th group,\n- $\\overline{x}$ is the grand mean.\n\nNow, if $s_k^2$ is the unbiased estimate of the population variance $\\sigma^2$ obtained from the data in group $k$ only, then\n\n$$\ns_k^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n_k-1}\\sum_{j=1}^{n_k}\\left(x_{jk} - \\overline{x}_k\\right)^2 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (n_k-1)s_k^2 = \\sum_{j=1}^{n_k}\\left(x_{jk} - \\overline{x}_k\\right)^2\n$$\n\nThis means we can express $\\text{SSW}$ in the following simplified form:\n\n$$\n\\text{SSW} = \\sum_{k=1}^K (n_k - 1)s_k^2\n$$\n\nMoreover, the total sum of squares is\n\n$$\n\\text{SST} = (n-1)s^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $n$ is the grand sample size,\n- $s^2$ is the grand (unbiased) estimate of the population variance $\\sigma^2$.\n\nIn our case, since there are $3$ groups, we have $K=3$, and we can compute SSW as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSSW &= (20 - 1)(3.2)^{2} + (24 - 1)(3.4)^{2} + (20 - 1)(3.8)^{2} \\\\\n&= 194.56 + 265.88 + 274.36 \\\\\n&= 734.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\nn = 20+24+20 = 64\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\text{SST} = (64-1)\\cdot (15)=945\n$$\n\nNow, let's now examine our statements:\n\n- Computing the given ratio, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\text{SSW}}{\\text{SST}} = \\dfrac{734.8}{945}\\approx \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}78\\%}.\n$$\n- We're interested in determining the between-group variability. First, recall that\n$\\textrm{SSW} + \\textrm{SSB} = \\textrm{SST}$\nand therefore\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\textrm{SSW}}{\\textrm{SST}} + \\dfrac{\\textrm{SSB}}{\\textrm{SST}} = 1.\n$$\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{SSB}}{\\text{SST}} & =1-\\frac{\\text{SSW}}{\\text{SST}} \\\\  & \\approx 100\\mathrm{\\%}-78\\mathrm{\\%} \\\\  & =22\\mathrm{\\%} \\\\  & <40\\mathrm{\\%}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince a low proportion of the variability is *between* groups, relatively high variability is attributable to differences *within* the group means. In other words, this suggests the between-group variability is $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{low}}$."] [17592186204245 :question/problem "Consider the following relations defined on $X = \\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =\\{(1,3),(2,1),(2,3),(3,4)\\} \\\\ S & =\\{(2,1),(3,1),(3,2)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nExpress the composition $S∘R$ as a list of ordered pairs.$S∘R = \\{(1, 1), (1$,\n{{field-1}} $), ($\n{{field-2}} $, 1), ($\n{{field-3}} $, 2)\\}$"] [17592186204294 :question/problem "Consider the following relations defined on $R$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}y=\\operatorname{ln}x\\} \\\\ S & =\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}:\\hspace{0.167em}x={y}^{2}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following pairs are elements of the relation $S∘R$?\n\n1. $(1, 0)$\n2. $(e^{2}, \\sqrt{2})$\n3. $(e^{2},-\\sqrt{2})$\n4. $(e^{-1},-1)$"] [17592186205085 :tutorial/content "![](images/f5/f5a9529645599eb14fba81d451610c313f2b61e28d76a3d766ac60bc724155ce.png)\n\nWe wish to prove the following statement:\n\n> *$\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ are parallel.*\n\nNotice that, according to the given information, the angles $\\angle{AQR}$ and $\\angle{QRC}$ are supplementary. Thus, the statement we want to prove is the converse consecutive interior angles theorem!\n\nOur strategy is to use the fact that $\\angle AQR$ and $\\angle AQP$ form a linear pair (and are therefore supplementary) and show this implies that the corresponding angles $\\angle AQP$ and $\\angle QRC$ must be congruent. By the converse corresponding angles postulate, this is enough to prove that the lines are parallel.\n\nWe can divide the proof into steps as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: We are *given* that $m\\angle{AQR=\\alpha}$ and $m\\angle{QRC}= 180^\\circ - \\alpha$.\n- **Step 2**: By the *linear pair postulate*, $\\angle{AQR}$ and $\\angle{AQP}$ are supplementary. Let's add this to our diagram.\n\n![](images/84/840e5eba9781e522675f337a4ba9159e69b754a85a9f7178e1839efecfa82ce1.png)\n\n- **Step 3**: Since $\\angle{AQR}$ and $\\angle{AQP}$ are supplementary, we have\n$m\\angle{AQR} + m\\angle{AQP} = 180^\\circ$\nby the *definition of supplemetary angles*.\n- **Step 4**: Using the *addition principle*, we can subtract $m\\angle AQR$ from both sides of the equality from step 3. This gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}AQR+m\\mathrm{∠}AQP & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}AQR+m\\mathrm{∠}AQP-m\\mathrm{∠}AQR & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}AQR \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}AQP & ={180}^{∘}-m\\mathrm{∠}AQR\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Step 5**: From step 1, we have\n$m\\angle{AQR} = \\alpha$.\nTherefore, by *substituting* this into the result from step 4, we get\n$m\\angle AQP = 180^\\circ -\\alpha$.\n\n- **Step 6**: From step 1, we have\n$m\\angle QRC = 180^\\circ -\\alpha$,\nand in step 5, we showed that\n$m\\angle AQP = 180^\\circ -\\alpha$.\nTherefore, by the *transitive property of equality*, we have\n$m\\angle AQP = m\\angle{QRC}$.\n- **Step 7**: Using the *definition of congruent angles*, we get\n$\\angle AQP \\cong \\angle{QRC}$.Let's add this to our diagram.\n\n![](images/79/797e5aa86f7174f6858c84609c6cb55605191af7b25f24dc9f6701bfe9904f8d.png)\n\n- **Step 8**: Finally, note that $\\angle AQP$ and $\\angle{QRC}$ are corresponding angles, and they are congruent from step 7. Consequently, by the *converse corresponding angles postulate,* *$\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ are parallel,* as required.\n\nIt's **highly recommended** that you practice reconstructing this proof. Try to do this *without* referring to these notes."] [17592186205101 :tutorial/content "Suppose the angles $\\angle BAC$ and $\\angle CAD$ are congruent.\n\n![](images/67/67f3dd53dfd7ef483b7c66cc79656090b26ebb7c633c847c2364a51ef476f670.png)\n\nWe wish to prove the following statement:\n\n> *The lines $l$ and $m$ are perpendicular.*\n\nOur strategy for proving this result goes like this: By the linear pair postulate, the two angles are supplementary. But since they're also equal, both angles must equal $90^\\circ$. Therefore, the two lines are perpendicular.\n\nLet's now construct our step-by-step proof:\n\n- **Step 1**: We are given that $\\angle BAC\\cong \\angle CAD$.\n- **Step 2**: By the definition of *congruent angles*, we have that $m\\angle BAC= m\\angle CAD$.\n- **Step 3**: By the *linear pair postulate* $\\angle BAC$ and $\\angle CAD$ are supplementary.\n- **Step 4**: By the definition of *supplementary angles*, it follows that $m\\angle BAC+m\\angle CAD= 180^\\circ$.\n- **Step 5**: In steps 2 and 4 respectively, we showed that\n$$\n\\displaystyle m\\angle BAC =m\\angle CAD\\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad m\\angle BAC +m\\angle CAD= 180^\\circ.\n$$\nTherefore, using *substitution*, we obtain that\n$\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}CAD+m\\mathrm{∠}CAD & ={180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}$\n- **Step 6**: Using the result of step 5, we solve for $m\\angle CAD{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}CAD+m\\mathrm{∠}CAD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m\\mathrm{∠}CAD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}CAD & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Step 7**: Finally, by the *definition of perpendicular lines*, we conclude that $l$ and $m$ are perpendicular, as required."] [17592186205116 :tutorial/content "![](images/03/03cb9673fbe7cc21c747f1abe3f735ede5aebfaa81361b24f144ef4ba9d9ded4.png)\n\n$$\n\\overline{RJ}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overline{SI}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overline{TL}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\triangle{RST}\n$$\n\n$$\nG\n$$\n\nIn a previous lesson, we saw that the coordinates of $G$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\left(x_G,y_G\\right) = \\left(\\dfrac{x_1+x_2+x_3}{3}, \\dfrac{y_1+y_2+y_3}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe can prove this result by partitioning the line segment $\\overline{RJ}$ in the ratio $2:1$ as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: Use the *midpoint formula* to find the coordinates of $J$ in terms of the coordinates of $S$ and $T$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{J} & =\\frac{{x}_{S}+{x}_{T}}{2} \\\\ {y}_{J} & =\\frac{{y}_{S}+{y}_{T}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Step 2**: Partition the segment $\\overline{RJ}$ in the ratio $2:1$. To do this, we can use the following formulas (derived in a previous lesson).\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{G} & ={x}_{R}+\\frac{2}{3}({x}_{J}-{x}_{R}) \\\\ {y}_{G} & ={y}_{R}+\\frac{2}{3}({y}_{J}-{y}_{R})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Step 3**: Use the result from step 1 to write $x_G$ and $y_G$ in terms of the coordinates of the vertices only.\n\nLet's now look at the full proof."] [17592186205121 :tutorial/content "The first theorem we'll consider is the following:\n\n> *If a parallelogram has congruent diagonals, then it's a rectangle.*\n\nTo prove this theorem, consider the diagram below, where $PQRS$ is a parallelogram and $\\overline{QS} \\cong \\overline{PR}$.\n\n![](images/14/146f01f2b906ce0a0b635a31f34ca466b56b5f69f409aa65028fa758808767e5.png)\n\nWe wish to prove the following statement:\n\n> $\\angle{QPS}$ is a right angle\n\nAs we'll see shortly, this is enough to conclude that $PQRS$ is a rectangle.\n\nFirst, let's outline our strategy for this proof:\n\n- First, we want to show that $\\triangle QPS$ and $\\triangle RSP$ are congruent using the SSS congruence criterion.\n- Next, we'll CPCTC to deduce that $\\angle QPS \\cong \\angle RSP$.\n- Finally, $\\angle QPS$ and $\\angle RSP$ are also supplementary since $PQRS$ is a parallelogram. This gives us enough information to deduce that $\\angle QPS$ must be a right angle.\n\nFollowing this strategy, we complete our proof as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: We are *given* that $PQRS$ is a parallelogram.\n- **Step 2**: Since $PQRS$ is a parallelogram, *its opposite sides are congruent*. Therefore, we have\n$\\overline{PQ} \\cong \\overline{RS}$.\n\n![](images/11/114a8278e0f96dfc3a4fd8b8d769c077db015baf84d0668593be51e5358d22f0.png)\n\n- **Step 3**: We are *given* that $\\overline{QS} \\cong \\overline{PR}$.\n\n![](images/33/339a2ea64dd90242029920fc95916885373f42e7ec26e94934a65c3bbbc74484.png)\n\n- **Step 4**: The *reflexive property of congruence* states that every segment is congruent to itself. Therefore, $\\overline{PS} \\cong \\overline{PS}$.\n\n![](images/37/3717b641861d6d08b98b2d1a0e9b18fe906b829120dd15e542b79f2d1d8d3cfe.png)\n\n- **Step 5**: In steps 2, 3, and 4, we showed that all three sides of $\\triangle{QPS}$ are congruent to the corresponding sides of $\\triangle{RSP}$. Therefore,\n$\\triangle{QPS} \\cong \\triangle{RSP}$\nby the *SSS criterion*.\n- **Step 6**: Since $\\triangle{QPS} \\cong \\triangle{RSP}$, we have\n$\\angle{QPS} \\cong \\angle{RSP}$\nby *CPCTC*.\n\n![](images/81/81cfce99ad0623e151ae48841b9a2fc20142391c383c4758fe9f80d97cbe9740.png)\n\n- **Step 7**: By *the definition of congruent angles,* we obtain\n$m\\angle{QPS} = m\\angle{RSP}$.\n- **Step 8**: Since *adjacent angles of a parallelogram are supplementary,* we have that $\\angle{QPS}$ and $\\angle{RSP}$ are supplementary.\n- **Step 9**: Since $\\angle{QPS}$ and $\\angle{RSP}$ are supplementary, by the *definition of supplementary angles,* we have\n$m\\angle{QPS} + m\\angle{RSP} = 180^\\circ$.\n\n- **Step 10**: *Substituting* the expression from step 7 into the expression from step 9, we get\n$m\\angle{QPS} + m\\angle{QPS} = 180^\\circ$.\n- **Step 11**: We can solve for $m\\angle{QPS}$ using *algebra* as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\mathrm{∠}QPS+m\\mathrm{∠}QPS & ={180}^{∘}. \\\\ 2m\\mathrm{∠}QPS & ={180}^{∘}. \\\\ m\\mathrm{∠}QPS & ={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Step 12**: Finally, by the *definition of a right angle*, we conclude that $\\angle{QPS}$ is a right angle, as required.\n\nLet's now state our proof using a two-column format."] [17592186205140 :tutorial/content "To find the general solution to an inhomogeneous recurrence relation, we perform the following steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the general solution $a_n^{(h)}$ of the associated homogeneous recurrence relation.\n- **Step 2:** Find a particular solution $a_n^{(p)}$ to the inhomogeneous recurrence relation.\n- **Step 3:** Find the general solution of the inhomogeneous recurrence relation by adding the general solution of the homogeneous equation and the particular solution to the inhomogeneous equation:\n$a_n = a_n^{(h)} + a_n^{(p)}$\n\nTo illustrate, let's find the general solution to the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\na_n = -2a_{n-1} + 6, \\qquad n\\geq 2\n$$\n\nWe proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the general solution $a_n^{(h)}$ of the associated homogeneous recurrence relation.The associated homogeneous recurrence relation is\n$a_n = -2a_{n-1}, \\qquad n\\geq 2$\nand this has the general solution\n$a_n^{(h)} = A \\cdot (-2)^n, \\qquad n\\geq 1$.\nThe general solution of the homogeneous equation is called the **complementary solution** or **complementary function.**\n\n- **Step 2:** Find the particular solution $a_n^{(p)}$ that satisfies the inhomogeneous recurrence relation.Since the inhomogeneous function $g(n)=6$ is a polynomial of degree $0$, we assume the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0$, i.e.\n$a_n^{(p)} = \\alpha$,\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant. Since $a_n^{(p)}$ does not depend on $n$, we also have\n$a_{n-1}^{(p)} = \\alpha$.Substituting the above into the original relation, we obtain\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n}^{(p)} & =-2{a}_{n-1}^{(p)}+6 \\\\ {a}_{n}^{(p)}+2{a}_{n-1}^{(p)} & =6 \\\\ \\alpha +2\\alpha  & =6 \\\\ 3\\alpha  & =6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n$a_n^{(p)} = 2$.\n- **Step 3:** Find the general solution.To find the general solution, we simply sum $a_n^{(h)}$ and $a_n^{(p)}$. Therefore, our general solution is\n$a_n = A \\cdot (-2)^n + 2, \\qquad n\\geq 1$.\n\nFinally, note that if we're also given the first term of the recurrence relation, we can use this information to find the constant $A$. We'll see an example of this later in the lesson."] [17592186205149 :tutorial/content "It will be convenient to collect all the relevant statistics for this problem in a **one-factor ANOVA table,** shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Source</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{SS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,df\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{MS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,F\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Between Groups</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{SSB}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$K-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{MSB}=\\dfrac{\\text{SSB}}{K-1}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Within Groups</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{SSW}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$n-K$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{MSW}=\\dfrac{\\text{SSW}}{n-K}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Total</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\text{SST}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$n-1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nRecall that:\n\n- $\\text{SSW}$ is the sum of squares within groups,\n- $\\text{SSB}$ is the sum of squares between groups,\n- $\\text{SST}$ is the total sum of squares, and $\\text{SST}=\\text{SSW}+\\text{SSB}$,\n- $K$ is the number of groups,\n- $n$ is the total number of sample elements.\n\nFor example, suppose a particular feature is being examined across $5$ groups of individuals to determine if the mean varies by group.\nA sample of size $n=80$ is conducted and it is found that\n\n$$\n\\text{SSB} = 124, \\qquad \\text{SSW} = 262.5\n$$\n\nLet's complete the one-factor ANOVA table for this sample.\n\n- First, we are given $\\text{SSB}$ and $\\text{SSW}$, so we can compute $\\text{SST}$ as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SST} & =\\text{SSB}+\\text{SSW} \\\\  & =124+262.5 \\\\  & =386.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The degrees of freedom of $\\text{SSB}$ are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d{f}_{B} & =K-1 \\\\  & =5-1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The degrees of freedom of $\\text{SSW}$ are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d{f}_{W} & =n-K \\\\  & =80-5 \\\\  & =75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The degrees of freedom of $\\text{SST}$ are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d{f}_{T} & =n-1 \\\\  & =80-1 \\\\  & =79.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The mean sum of squares between groups is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{MSB} & =\\frac{\\text{SSB}}{K-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{5-1} \\\\  & =31.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The mean sum of squares within groups is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{MSW} & =\\frac{\\text{SSW}}{n-K} \\\\  & =\\frac{262.5}{80-5} \\\\  & =3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Finally, the $F$ ratio is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\text{MSB}}{\\text{MSW}} = \\dfrac{31}{3.5} \\approx 8.857.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as follows.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">Source</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{SS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,df\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,\\textrm{MS}\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,F\\,$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Between</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$124$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$31$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$8.857$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Within</td> <td data-cell-align=\"center\">$262.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$75$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">Total</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$386.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$79$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186205224 :tutorial/content "We can use proof by contradiction to prove that certain numbers are irrational.\n\nFor example, let's show that $\\sqrt{2}$ is an irrational number. We begin by stating the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\n\\phantom{\\lnot} \\:\\: P: \\quad { {\\sqrt{2}} \\textrm{ is irrational}}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P: \\quad { {\\sqrt{2}} \\textrm{ is rational}}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> *Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\sqrt{2}$ is rational.*\n\nIf a number is rational, it can be written as a fraction in its lowest terms, i.e., where the numerator and denominator are coprime.\n\n> *Then,*\n> $\\sqrt{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$\n> *for some integers $a$ and $b$, where $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.*\n\nThe contradiction we seek is that $a$ and $b$ are, in fact, *not* coprime under this assumption.\n\nWe start by squaring both sides of the equation:\n\n> *So, we have*\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{a}{b} \\\\ (\\sqrt{2})^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{{a}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} \\\\ 2{b}^{2} & ={a}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> *which, by the definition of divisibility, means that $2 \\mid a^2$.*\n\nNow, recall the following property of divisibility by a prime $p{:}$\n$p \\mid xy \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (p \\mid x) \\lor (p \\mid y)$\nApplying this property with $p=2$ and $x=y=a$, we have\n\n$$\n2 \\mid a\\cdot a = a^2 \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad (2 \\mid a) \\lor (2 \\mid a)\n$$\n\nSo, we have that $2 \\mid a$.\n\n> *Since $2$ is prime, we have $2 \\mid a$, which means $a = 2c$ where $c$ is an integer.*\n\nNow, we substitute $a=2c$ into the equation $2b^2 = a^2$ and show that $2$ is *also* a factor of $b$.\n\n> *Therefore,*\n> $\\begin{aligned}2{b}^{2} & =(2c)^{2} \\\\ 2{b}^{2} & =4{c}^{2} \\\\ {b}^{2} & =2{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}$\n> *Hence, by the definition of divisibility, $2 \\mid b^2$, which means that $2 \\mid b$.*\n\nFinally, since $a$ and $b$ have a common factor of $2$, their greatest common divisor is at least $2$. This gives us our contradiction.\n\n> *Combining the results $2 \\mid a$ and $2 \\mid b$, we conclude that $\\gcd(a,b) \\geq 2$. But this contradicts the assumption that $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.*\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> *Therefore, $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational.*\n\nNow that we've figured out the details, let's state the full proof."] [17592186205269 :tutorial/content "We can use proof by contradiction to prove the following properties about rational and irrational numbers:\n\n- The sum of a rational and an irrational number is always irrational.\n- The product of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is always irrational.\n- The quotient of an irrational number and a nonzero rational number is always irrational.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to prove these properties in particular cases.\n\nFor example, assume we've proved that $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational. Let's use this result to prove that $2\\sqrt{3}$ is also irrational.\n\nFirst, we write down the formal statement $P$ that we wish to prove:\n\n$$\n\\phantom{\\lnot}\\:\\: P{:} \\quad 2\\sqrt{3} \\: \\textrm{is irrational}\n$$\n\nTo prove that $P$ is true, we assume that $\\lnot P$ is true and show this leads to a contradiction.\n\n$$\n\\lnot P{:} \\quad 2\\sqrt{3} \\: \\textrm{is rational}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows:\n\n> *Assume, for a contradiction, that $2\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.*\n\nSince we're assuming $2\\sqrt{3}$ is rational, we can express it as the ratio of two nonzero integers:\n\n> *Then $2\\sqrt{3} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.*\n\nThe idea is to rewrite this equation to give a radical $(\\sqrt 3)$ on one side and a fraction on the other. This will provide us with a contradiction since $\\sqrt 3$ is irrational and, therefore, cannot equal a ratio of two integers.\n\n> *So, we have*\n> $\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{a}{b} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{a}{\\underbrace{2b}_{c}}.\\end{aligned}$.\n\nNow, if we define the integer $c=2b$ (highlighted above), we have that\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}=\\dfrac{a}{c}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.\n\n> *Let $c=2b$. Since $2$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.*\n> *Therefore, we have*\n> $\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{a}{c}$,\n> *which is rational.*\n> *But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.*\n\nSince we have a contradiction, our original assumption must be incorrect. Hence, we write our conclusion as follows:\n\n> *Therefore, $2\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.*\n\nNow that we've figured out the details, let's state the full proof."] [17592186205271 :tutorial/content "We wish to prove that $A \\subseteq B$, where the sets $A$ and $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nA = \\{6m-4 \\:: \\: m \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\}, \\qquad B = \\{2n \\:: \\: n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\}\n$$\n\nWe begin our proof as follows.\n\n> *To prove that $A \\subseteq B$, we take an arbitrary element of $A$ and show that it is an element of $B$.*\n\nNow, we denote an arbitrary element of $A$ by $x$ and use the definition of $A$.\n\n> *So, let $x \\in A$. Then, according to the definition of $A$, there exists $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ such that*\n> $x = 6m-4$.\n\nThe key to unlocking the proof is to rewrite $x$ to match the definition of $B$. In other words, we need to write $x=2n$, where $n$ is an integer.\n\n> *Note that*\n> $\\begin{aligned}x & =6m-4 \\\\  & =2(3m-2).\\end{aligned}$.\n> *Denote $n=3m-2$. Since $m$ is an integer, $n$ is also an integer. Thus,*\n> $x=2n$,\n> *where $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. So, by the definition of $B$, we have $x \\in B$.*\n\nSo, we have shown that $x\\in A$ implies $x \\in B$, and we can state our conclusion.\n\n> *Therefore, $A \\subseteq B$.*"] [17592186205275 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n\n> *Given that $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational, prove that $2\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.*\n\nProof:\n\n> *Assume, for a contradiction, that $2\\sqrt{3}$ is rational.*\n> *Then $2\\sqrt{3} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$ for some non-zero integers $a$ and $b$.*\n> *So, we have*\n> $\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{a}{b} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\frac{a}{\\underbrace{2b}_{c}}.\\end{aligned}$.\n> *Let $c=2b$. Since $2$ and $b$ are integers, we have that $c$ is an integer.*\n> *Therefore, we have*\n> $\\sqrt{3} = \\frac{a}{c}$,\n> *which is rational.*\n> *But this is a contradiction since $\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.*\n> *Therefore, $2\\sqrt{3}$ is irrational.*\n\nThe whole proof rests on the assumption that $\\sqrt 3$ is irrational. You may ask, \"But how do we know that $\\sqrt 3$ is irrational?\"\n\nThe irrationality of $\\sqrt 3$ can be proved using proof by contradiction! However, the proof methodology is slightly different, so we'll save that discussion for a separate lesson."] [17592186205276 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n\n> *$\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational*\n\nProof:\n\n> *Assume, for a contradiction, that $\\sqrt{2}$ is rational. Then,*\n> $\\sqrt{2} = \\dfrac{a}{b}$\n> *for some integers $a$ and $b$, where $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.*\n> *So, we have*\n> $$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{a}{b} \\\\ (\\sqrt{2})^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{{a}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} \\\\ 2{b}^{2} & ={a}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n> *which means that $2 \\mid a^2$.*\n> *Since $2$ is prime, we have $2 \\mid a$, which means $a = 2c$ where $c$ is an integer.*\n> *Therefore,*\n> $\\begin{aligned}2{b}^{2} & =(2c)^{2} \\\\ 2{b}^{2} & =4{c}^{2} \\\\ {b}^{2} & =2{c}^{2}\\end{aligned}$\n> *Hence, by the definition of divisibility, $2 \\mid b^2$, which means that $2 \\mid b$.*\n> *Combining the results $2 \\mid a$ and $2 \\mid b$, we conclude that $\\gcd(a,b) \\geq 2$. But this contradicts the assumption that $\\gcd(a,b) = 1$.*\n> *Therefore, $\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational.*"] [17592186205278 :tutorial/content "Proposition:\n\n> *Given that $A = \\{6m-4 \\:: \\: m \\in \\mathbb{N} \\}$ and $B = \\{2n \\:: \\: n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\}$, prove that $A \\subseteq B$.*\n\nProof:\n\n> *To prove that $A \\subseteq B$, we take an arbitrary element of $A$ and show that it is an element of $B$.*\n> *So, let $x \\in A$. Then, according to the definition of $A$, there exists $m \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that*\n> $x = 6m-4$.\n> *Note that*\n> $\\begin{aligned}x & =6m-4 \\\\  & =2(3m-2).\\end{aligned}$.\n> *Denote $n=3m-2$. Since $m$ is an integer, $n$ is also an integer. Thus,*\n> $x=2n$,\n> *where $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. So, by the definition of $B$, we have $x \\in B$.*\n> *Therefore, $A \\subseteq B$.*"] [17592186208307 :question/worked-solution "To write the table, we first express $y$ as a function of $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{4}y &= -5 - \\frac{1}{2}x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{4}{3}(-5 - \\frac{1}{2}x) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{20}{3} - \\frac{2}{3}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we’ll analyze each of the given values of $x$ in turn.\n\n- Consider $x = 0$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{20}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}(0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider $x = 3$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{20}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}(3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{3}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{26}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Consider $x = 6$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{20}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}(6) \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{3}-4 \\\\  & =-\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the table is the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle -\\dfrac{20}{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle -\\dfrac{26}{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\displaystyle -\\dfrac{32}{3}$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186208340 :question/worked-solution "We are given that the circle has its center at $(1,1)$ and that line $k$ is tangent to this circle at the point $(3,-1)$. Therefore, the segment connecting the center $(1,1)$ with the point of tangency $(3,-1)$ is a circle's radius.\n\nThe slope of a line (or line segment) passing through points $(x_1,y_1)$ and $(x_2, y_2)$ is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}\n$$\n\nThen, the slope of the radius with endpoints at $(1,1)$ and $(3,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{-1 - 1}{3 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-2}{2} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that a line is tangent to a circle if and only if it's perpendicular to the radius drawn to the point of tangency. Consequently, $k$ is perpendicular to the radius at the point $(3, -1)$. Hence, the slope of $k$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of this radius, that is, the slope of $k$ is $1$.\n\nSince the slope of line $k$ is the same between any two points on line $k$, a point lies on line $k$ if the slope of the line segment connecting the point and $(3,-1)$ is $1$. Taking this into account, we proceed to verify which of the given points is on the line $k$.\n\n- The slope of the line segment with endpoints at $(1,-2)$ and $(3,-1)$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{-1-(-2)}{3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & \\ne 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, $(1,-2)$ does not lie on the line $k$.\n- The slope of the line segment with endpoints at $(5,1)$ and $(3,-1)$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{-1-1}{3-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{-2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, $(5,1)$ lies on the line $k$.\n\n- The slope of the line segment with endpoints at $(0,-1)$ and $(3,-1)$ is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{-1-(-1)}{3-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{3} \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, $(0,-1)$ does not lie on the line $k$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$(5,1)$ only.\""] [17592186208361 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we can inscribe our triangle within the rectangle shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b6/b6fd666615d54950f20c7b5ca219de1e3a1867f3fb989370ef4d9ff1e7e9f328.png)\n\nWe have the rectangle, whose area we denote by $A$, and four triangles:\n\n- $\\triangle ABC$, whose area $A_{\\triangle}$ we're required to determine,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$ and area $A_1$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$ and area $A_2$, and\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$ and area $A_3$.\n\nRecall that the area of a figure consisting of several smaller non-overlapping parts is equal to the sum of the areas of those parts. So, we have\n\n$$\nA = A_{\\triangle} + A_1 + A_2 + A_3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = A - A_1 - A_2 - A_3\n$$\n\nLet's now compute all the areas on the right-hand side of this equation.\n\n- For the area of the rectangle, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\text{width}\\cdot \\text{height} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{1}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{1}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{1}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = 20 - 2 - 6 - 2.5 = 9.5\n$$\n\nsquare units."] [17592186208391 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the absolute value terms contain the same expression. So, by combining these like terms, the equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3|4-a|-7|4-a| & =-60 \\\\ -4|4-a| & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, dividing both sides by $-4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mid 4 - a \\mid = 15\n$$\n\nNext, we consider both possible cases for the absolute value.\n\n- If $\\mid 4 - a \\mid = 4 - a$, then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4-a| & =15 \\\\ 4-a & =15 \\\\ -a & =11 \\\\ a & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- If $\\mid 4 - a \\mid = -(4 - a)$, then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4-a| & =15 \\\\ -(4-a) & =15 \\\\ -4+a & =15 \\\\ a & =19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the question asks for the positive solution, the correct answer is\n\n$$\na=\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{19}\n$$"] [17592186208392 :question/worked-solution "To solve this equation, we consider both possible cases for the absolute value.\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left|\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right| = \\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6},\n$$ then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6}| & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6} & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{14}{6}+\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{25}{6} \\\\ x & =\\frac{25}{6}\\cdot \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{125}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left|\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right| = -\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}x - \\dfrac{11}{6}\\right),\n$$ then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6}| & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -(\\frac{3}{5}x-\\frac{11}{6}) & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x+\\frac{11}{6} & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{7}{3}-\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{14}{6}-\\frac{11}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}x & =\\frac{3}{6} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{6}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{5}{3}\\right) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{15}{18} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{125}{18} + \\left(-\\dfrac{5}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{125}{18} - \\dfrac{15}{18} = \\dfrac{110}{18} = \\dfrac{55}{9}\n$$"] [17592186208393 :question/worked-solution "First, we isolate the absolute value by adding $11$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid s - \\frac{7}{6} \\mid - 11 &= -5 \\\\\n\\mid s - \\frac{7}{6} \\mid &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we consider both possible cases for the absolute value.\n\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left| s - \\dfrac{7}{6} \\right| = s - \\dfrac{7}{6},\n$$ then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|s-\\frac{7}{6}| & =6 \\\\ s-\\frac{7}{6} & =6 \\\\ s & =\\frac{7}{6}+6 \\\\ s & =\\frac{7}{6}+\\frac{36}{6} \\\\ s & =\\frac{43}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- If $$\n\\displaystyle \\left| s - \\dfrac{7}{6} \\right| = -\\!\\left(s - \\dfrac{7}{6} \\right),\n$$ then\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|s-\\frac{7}{6}| & =6 \\\\ -\\hspace{-0.167em}(s-\\frac{7}{6}) & =6 \\\\ -s+\\frac{7}{6} & =6 \\\\ -s & =6-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ -s & =\\frac{36}{6}-\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ -s & =\\frac{29}{6} \\\\ s & =-\\frac{29}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{43}{6} + \\left(-\\dfrac{29}{6}\\right) = \\dfrac{14}{6} = \\dfrac{7}{3}\n$$"] [17592186208406 :question/worked-solution "To determine the correct table, we calculate $z(x)$ for each of the given values of $x$.\n\n- For $x=1$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z(1) & ={1}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =1+5 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For $x=4$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z(4) & ={4}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =16+5 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For $x=5$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z(5) & ={5}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =25+5 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct table is the following.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th>\n<th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$z(x)$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$21$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$30$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186208511 :question/worked-solution "We want to find the best model for the scatterplot data shown, in the quadratic form\n\n$$\ny = ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nwhere $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants, with\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\n- The data in the scatterplot appear to form a downward-opening parabola. Therefore, the leading coefficient $a$ must be negative.\n- The points look symmetric about the line $x=4$. Using the axis of symmetry formula $x=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}$ and solving for $b$, we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{b}{2a} \\\\ 4 & =-\\frac{b}{2a} \\\\ b & =-8a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $a$ is negative, the coefficient of the linear term should be positive.\n- The $y$-intercept of the quadratic model is near $(0,650)$.\n\nTherefore, of the given choices, only choice\n\n$$\ny = -2.28x^{2} + 20.2x+652.76\n$$\n\nis the best quadratic model for the data in the scatterplot."] [17592186212771 :question/worked-solution "![](images/60/609738e75d7d263268af65470237126cd34eb808e63c497fda68814f3cb82598.png)\n\nWe have the rectangle, whose area we denote by $A_{\\square}$, and four triangles:\n\n- $\\triangle ABC$, whose area $A_{\\triangle}$ we're required to determine,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$ and area $A_1$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$ and area $A_2$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$ and area $A_3$.\n\nRecall that the area of a figure consisting of several smaller non-overlapping parts is equal to the sum of the areas of those parts. So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\square} = A_{\\triangle} + A_1 + A_2 + A_3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = A_{\\square} - A_1 - A_2 - A_3\n$$\n\nLet's now compute all the areas on the right-hand side of this equation.\n\n- For the area of the rectangle, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{◻} & =\\text{width}\\cdot \\text{height} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =54\\hspace{0.222em}{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{1}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =13.5\\hspace{0.222em}{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{1}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9\\hspace{0.222em}{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{1}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =9\\hspace{0.222em}{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = 54 - 13.5 - 9 - 9 = 22.5 \\: \\text{cm}^2\n$$"] [17592186212772 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we can inscribe our triangle within the rectangle shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/034a8b7157f2b08f41d5531325ecce2205413dc02410852900046fe593e017e1.png)\n\nWe have the rectangle, whose area we denote by $A_{\\square}$, and four triangles:\n\n- $\\triangle ABC$, whose area $A_{\\triangle}$ we're required to determine,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$ and area $A_1$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$ and area $A_2$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$ and area $A_3$.\n\nRecall that the area of a figure consisting of several smaller non-overlapping parts is equal to the sum of the areas of those parts. So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\square} = A_{\\triangle} + A_1 + A_2 + A_3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = A_{\\square} - A_1 - A_2 - A_3\n$$\n\nLet's now compute all the areas on the right-hand side of this equation.\n\n- For the area of the rectangle, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{◻} & =\\text{width}\\cdot \\text{height} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{1}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{1}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =10.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{1}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = 42 - 6 - 10.5 - 7.5 = 18\n$$\n\nsquare units."] [17592186212818 :question/worked-solution "We're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nand want to convert it into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nTo do this, we must apply two conversion factors: one for the area units (from $\\textrm{yd}^2$ to $\\textrm{ft}^2$) and one for the time units (from $\\textrm{min}$ to $\\textrm{s}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{yd}^2$ to $\\textrm{ft}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}}{\\textrm{min}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{min}$ to $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{min}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{yd}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{min}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{yd}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{min}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given the quantity $33\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}}$.\n- There are $3$ feet per yard. We can write this as follows:\n$3\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}}{\\textrm{yd}}$\nTo determine the number of square feet in one square yard, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(3\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{ft}}{\\text{yd}})}^{2} & ={3}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{ft}}^{2}}{{\\text{yd}}^{2}} \\\\  & =9\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{ft}}^{2}}{{\\text{yd}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- There are $60$ seconds per minute, or $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ minutes per second. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{60}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{min}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 33 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{yd}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} & \\displaystyle  = 33 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{yd}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} \\cdot  9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{ {\\text{yd}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 33 \\cdot  9 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} ) {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{yd}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{yd}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{297}{60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 4.95 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n33\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{yd}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to\n\n$$\n4.95\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$"] [17592186212819 :question/worked-solution "We're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert it to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nWe'll need to multiply by two conversion factors to change both the area unit ($\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and the time unit ($\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{ft}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{min}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{ft}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{ft}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- The robot's clearing rate is given as $132{,}000 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{ft}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- We are given that there are $3.28$ feet per meter. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3.28}$ meters per foot. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{3.28} \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{ft}}\n$$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square foot, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{3.28}\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{ft}})}^{2} & =\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{3.28}^{2}}\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{ft}}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10.7584}\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{ft}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- There are $60$ minutes per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ hours per minute. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{60} \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 132 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  \\approx  132 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10.7584}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 132 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{10.7584}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{60}} ) \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{ft}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{132 , 000}{10.7584 \\cdot  60}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{132 , 000}{645.504}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  204 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{min}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, the park maintenance robot is clearing leaves at a rate of approximately\n\n$$\n204\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{min}}\n$$"] [17592186212823 :question/worked-solution "Let's compute the medians for the datasets in turn.\n\n- The total number of values for store $A$ is $20$. Since our data set has an even number of data points, the median is the average of the two middle values in the positions\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{20}{2} = 10and10 + 1 = 11.\n$$\nNow, let’s determine where the $10$th and $11$th values fall. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in the left table, we see the following:Up to the second row, the table covers $7$ positions in total.Up to the third row, the table covers $11$ positions in total.So, the $10$th and $11$th values both fall into the third row corresponding to the value $35$ (items purchased). Therefore, the median is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Median} & =\\frac{35+35}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{70}{2} \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- The total number of values for store $B$ is $27$. Since our data set has an odd number of data points, the median is the value in position\n$\\frac{27 + 1}{2} = 14$.\nNow, let’s determine where the $14$th value falls. Looking at the cumulative frequency column in the right table, we see the following:Up to the second row, the table covers $8$ positions.Up to the third row, the table covers $23$ positions.So, the $14$th value falls into the third row corresponding to the value $38$ (items purchased). Therefore, the median is\n$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Median} & =38.\\end{aligned}$.\n\nAs a result, we conclude that the median of store A is smaller than the median of store B."] [17592186212855 :question/worked-solution "A system of linear equations has no solution if the equations represent distinct parallel lines. This occurs when both equations have the same slope but different $y$-intercepts.\n\nLet's start by rewriting the equations in slope-intercept form,\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is the slope and $b$ is the $y$-intercept.\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9+4y & =tx \\\\ 4y & =tx+9 \\\\ y & =\\frac{t}{4}x+\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, the slope is $\\dfrac{t}{4}$ and the $y$-intercept is $\\dfrac{9}{4}$.\n- For the second equation, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+x & =-5x+8 \\\\ 3y & =-6x+8 \\\\ y & =-2x+\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, the slope is $-2$ and the $y$-intercept is $\\dfrac{8}{3}$.\n\nSince the equations have different $y$-intercepts\n\n$$\n\\bigg(\\dfrac{9}{4}\\ \\text{and}\\ \\dfrac{8}{3}\\bigg)\n$$\n\nthe slopes must be equal for the system to have no solution.\n\nTherefore, equating the slopes and solving for $t$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{t}{4} &= -2 \\\\\nt &= -2 \\cdot 4 \\\\\nt &= -8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $t$ is $-8$."] [17592186212972 :question/worked-solution "We are given the units\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}\n$$\n\nand we want to convert this into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nWe'll need two conversion factors: one to convert the distance unit ($\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$), and one to convert the squared time unit ($\\textrm{s}^2$ to $\\textrm{min}^2$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{m}$ to $\\textrm{mi}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{s}^2$ to $\\textrm{min}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{m}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{s}^2}}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{s}^2}{\\textrm{min}^2} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given the quantity $5.1\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}^2}$.\n- There are $1,609$ meters in a mile. Taking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{1,609}$ miles per meter. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{1,609}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n- There are $60\\,\\textrm{s}$ per $\\textrm{min}$. We can write this as follows:\n$60\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{s}}{\\textrm{min}}$\nTo determine the number of square seconds in one square minute, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60\\hspace{0.167em}{\\left(\\frac{\\text{s}}{\\text{min}}\\right)}^{2} & ={60}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{s}}^{2}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}} \\\\  & =3,600\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{s}}^{2}}{{\\text{min}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 5.1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} & \\displaystyle  = 5.1 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{m}}{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\text{m}}} \\cdot  3 , 600 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 5.1 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{1 , 609}} \\cdot  3 , 600 ) {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{s}}^{2}}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{5.1 \\cdot  3 , 600}{1 , 609}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  \\approx  11.4 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{mi}}{ {\\text{min}}^{2}}} ,\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nTherefore, the train is accelerating at a rate of approximately\n\n$$\n11.4\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{mi}}{\\textrm{min}^2}\n$$"] [17592186214224 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll explore how to find the area of an arbitrary triangle in the coordinate plane.\n\nConsider $\\triangle ABC$ with vertices\n\n$$\nA(2,3), \\qquad B(6,7), \\qquad C(8,2)\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb6767a162efb99bf3db6e7961a15bf1c7af79a94b6ef253afdc341f2f92b27.png)\n\nFinding the area of this triangle using the usual methods is tricky because it's not easy to determine the length of the perpendicular height to a given base. So instead, we will proceed using an indirect method.\n\nFirst, we notice that we can **inscribe** our triangle within the rectangle shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b57fe9e05e0afa8228a9b5f0cbb65a14109660457880a4f461d0689e7593569.png)\n\nInscribing a triangle in a rectangle means drawing a rectangle around the triangle in such a way that the triangle lies completely inside the rectangle and each of the triangles vertices touches the rectangle.\n\nWe have the rectangle, whose area we denote by $A_{\\square}$, and four triangles:\n\n- $\\triangle ABC$, whose area $A_{\\triangle}$ we're required to determine,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$ and area that we denote by $A_1$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$ and area that we denote by $A_2$,\n- the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$ and area that we denote by $A_3$.\n\nRecall that the area of a figure consisting of several smaller non-overlapping parts is equal to the sum of the areas of those parts. So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\square} = A_{\\triangle} + A_1 + A_2 + A_3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = A_{\\square} - A_1 - A_2 - A_3\n$$\n\nIn other words, the area of the required (blue) triangle equals the area of the orange rectangle minus the areas of the three triangles with areas $A_1, A_2$, and $A_3$. Notice that it's much easier to find the areas of these three triangles compared to the blue triangle, since their bases and heights are easily determined from the diagram.\n\nLet's now compute all the areas on the right-hand side of this equation.\n\n- For the area of the rectangle, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{◻} & =\\text{width}\\cdot \\text{height} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AB}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{1}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{BC}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{1}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For the area of the right triangle with hypotenuse $\\overline{AC}$, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{1}\\cdot {\\text{leg}}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $\\triangle ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\triangle} = 30 - 8 - 5 - 3 = 14\n$$\n\nsquare units."] [17592186214229 :tutorial/content "Often in algebra, we’re so used to solving for a variable that we overlook faster, simpler paths to an answer. In many problems, the quantity we’re asked to find already contains a piece of information we’ve been given. Recognizing this can save time, reduce the possibility of errors, and sharpen our pattern-recognition skills.\n\nFor example, suppose we have the equation\n\n$$\n5y = 32\n$$\n\nand we want to determine the value of\n\n$$\n5y - 6\n$$\n\nWhat is the fastest way to do this? Let's compare two different methods.\n\n- **Method 1 (Slow)**The standard approach is to solve for $y$ using the first equation, and then substitute that value into the expression we want to evaluate.First, we solve for the variable $y{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y & =32 \\\\ \\frac{5y}{5} & =\\frac{32}{5} \\\\ y & =\\frac{32}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThen, we evaluate $5y-6{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-6 & =5\\cdot \\left(\\frac{32}{5}\\right)-6 \\\\  & =32-6 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- **Method 2 (Fast)**Notice that the term $\\color{blue}5y$ appears in *both* the equation *and* the expression we wish to evaluate. So, there's no need to solve for $y$. Instead, we can substitute ${\\color{blue}{5y}} = {\\color{red}{32}}$ directly into the expression ${\\color{blue}{5y}} - 6$, and evaluate:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\color{blue}{5y}}-6 & =({\\color{red}{32}})-6 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see some more examples."] [17592186214249 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw how to create and apply a conversion formula to convert quantities with mixed units, such as converting from \"kilometers per hour\" to \"meters per second.\"\n\nIn a separate lesson, we saw how to convert between different units of area, such as converting from \"square meters\" to \"square centimeters\" and vice versa.\n\nBy the end of this lesson, you'll be able to create and apply conversion formulas for mixed units that contain units of area.\n\nFor example, suppose a drone maps an area at a rate of\n\n$$\n0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nWhat is this rate in square meters per second?\n\nWe're given the rate in\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}\n$$\n\nand want to convert it into\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThis means we'll need two conversion factors: one for the area units (from $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$) and one for the time units (from $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$):\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}^2$ to $\\textrm{m}^2$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{h}$ to $\\textrm{s}$.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}}{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\cancel{\\textrm{km}^2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{h}}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, the complete conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{km}^2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use and apply them.\n\n- We are given that the drone maps at a rate of $0.9\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}^2}{\\textrm{h}}$.\n- There are $1,000$ meters in one kilometer. We can write this as follows:\n$1,000\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{km}}$\nTo determine the number of square meters in one square kilometer, we square this quantity and apply the rules of exponents, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1,000\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}})}^{2} & =1,{000}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{km}}^{2}} \\\\  & =1,000,000\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{\\text{m}}^{2}}{{\\text{km}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- There are $60\\cdot60=3,600$ seconds per hour, or $\\dfrac{1}{3,600}$ hours per second. We can write this as follows:\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{1}{3,600}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{h}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle 0.9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} & \\displaystyle  = 0.9 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}{\\text{h}}} \\cdot  1 , 000 , 000 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = ( 0.9 \\cdot  1 , 000 , 000 \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{3 , 600}} ) {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\cancel{ {\\text{km}}^{2}}}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{900 , 000}{3 , 600}} \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 250 \\hspace{0.167em} {\\displaystyle \\frac{ {\\text{m}}^{2}}{\\text{s}}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone maps the area at a rate of\n\n$$\n250\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}^2}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$"] [17592186216768 :question/worked-solution "When every data value is multiplied by a constant, each statistic responds as follows:\n\n- For $19$ *different* values, there is exactly one middle value (the $10$th value) of $50$. Multiplying every value by $2$ will also scale the middle value by $2$. So, the median changes.\n- The mean depends on every value, so multiplying every value by the same constant scales the mean by the same factor.If we denote the $19$ different totals by $t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_{19}$ and multiply each by $2$, then the new mean is:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}+⋯+2{t}_{19}}{19} & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({t}_{1}+{t}_{2}+⋯+{t}_{19})}{19} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 50 \\\\  & =100\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSo, the mean also changes.\n- The range depends only on the difference between the largest and the smallest values. Multiplying both by the same positive constant multiplies that difference by the same constant. Thus, the range is scaled by $2$, so it changes.\n- The standard deviation measures the spread about the mean. Since every value (and thus the mean) is multiplied by $2$, each deviation from the mean is also multiplied by $2$; hence the variance scales by $4$ and the standard deviation by $2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"."] [17592186216814 :question/worked-solution "Recall that by the factor theorem, if $(sx-r)$ is a factor of a polynomial $p(x)$, then\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{r}{s}\n$$\n\nis a root of the polynomial.\nThus, since $2x -5m$ is a factor of our polynomial $p(x)$, we must have\n\n$$\np\\left(\\dfrac{5m}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options in turn.\n\n- Option I, $p(x) = 4x^{2}+2x+18m$, is not valid. Here, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) & =4{\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)}^{2}+2\\hspace{-0.167em}\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)+18m \\\\  & =25{m}^{2}+5m+18m \\\\  & =25{m}^{2}+23m \\\\  & =m(25m+23).\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-\\dfrac{23}{25}$, neither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option II,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+10m\n$$\n\nis not valid. Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 10m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 15m \\\\\n&= 5m(5m + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nneither of which is a negative integer. ${\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n*Option III,*\n\n$$\np(x)=4x^{2}+2x+20m\n$$\n\nis valid. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\left(\\frac{3c}{2}\\right) &= 4\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right)^{2} + 2\\left(\\frac{5m}{2}\\right) + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 5m + 20m \\\\\n&= 25m^{2} + 25m \\\\\n&= 25m(m + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equals zero only at $m=0$ or $m=-1$, and $m=-1$ is a negative integer. ${\\color{darkgreen}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [17592186216819 :question/worked-solution "We know the vertex is at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (4, -8)\n$$\n\nSo, we can write the parabola in vertex form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= a(x - h)^{2} + k \\\\\ny &= a(x - 4)^{2} - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= a(x - 4)^{2} - 8 \\\\\n&= a(x^{2} - 8x + 16) - 8 \\\\\n&= ax^{2} - 8ax + 16a - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing it with the given standard quadratic form,\n\n$$\ny = ax^2 + bx + c\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na &= a \\\\\nb &= -8a \\\\\nc &= 16a - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, adding them up, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b + c &= a + (-8a) + (16a - 8) \\\\\n&= 9a - 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the parabola intersects the $x$-axis at two points and has its vertex at $(4, -8)$ below the $x$-axis, it must open upward, meaning $a > 0$.\n\nNow, let's go through all the options and check which ones give $a > 0{:}$\n\n- Option I, $-8{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9a-8 & =-8 \\\\ 9a & =-8+8 \\\\ 9a & =0 \\\\ a & =0≯0\\hspace{2em}{\\color{red}{\\times }}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Option II, $-1{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9a-8 & =-1 \\\\ 9a & =-1+8 \\\\ 9a & =7 \\\\ a & =\\frac{7}{9}>0\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Option III, $10{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9a-8 & =10 \\\\ 9a & =10+8 \\\\ 9a & =18 \\\\ a & =2>0\\hspace{2em}{\\color{green}{✓}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"."] [17592186216826 :question/worked-solution "We're given the equation of circle $\\text{C}$\n\n$$\n(x+3)^2+(y-2)^2=25\n$$\n\nand we wish to translate it by $4$ units right and double its radius.\n\nFor circle $\\text{C}{:}$\n\n- For each point on the circle, we must add $4$ to each $x$-coordinate to get the corresponding point on circle $\\text{D}$. So, the new $x$-coordinates of the points on circle $\\text{D}$ are given by\n$x_{\\textrm{new}} = x + 4$.\nThis equation is equivalent to\n$x = x_{\\textrm{new}} - 4$.\n- We also must double the radius of circle $\\text{C}$ to get the corresponding radius of circle $\\text{D}$. The right-hand side of the equation of circle $\\text{C}$ represents the square of its radius:\n$r^2 = 25$\nDoubling the radius of circle $\\text{C}$, the new right-hand side of the equation of circle $\\text{D}$ must be\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{\\text{new}} & =2r \\\\ {r}_{\\text{new}}^{2} & =(2r)^{2} \\\\  & =4{r}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 25 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get the equation of circle $\\text{D}$, we substitute into the equation of circle $\\text{C}$ and update the right-hand side accordingly to give the new squared radius. Doing so, we get\n\n$$\n((x_{new} - 4) + 3)^{2} + (y - 2)^{2} = 100\n$$\n\nDropping the $\\textrm{new}$ suffix, we get\n\n$$\n\\big((x - 4)+3\\big)^2+(y-2)^2=100\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying the expression, we have\n\n$$\n(x-1)^2+(y-2)^2=100\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of circle $\\text{D}$ is\n\n$$\n(x-1)^2+(y-2)^2=100\n$$"] [17592186216833 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(0,-1)$, we have $g(0) = -1$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $b{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0) & =-1 \\\\ {a}^{0}+b & =-1 \\\\ 1+b & =-1 \\\\ b & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Since the graph passes through the point $(3,25)$, we have $g(3) = 25$. Then, using the fact that $b=-2$, we can determine the value of $c$, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(3) & =25 \\\\ {a}^{3}+(-2) & =25 \\\\ {a}^{3}-2 & =25 \\\\ {a}^{3} & =27 \\\\ a & =\\sqrt[3]{27} \\\\ a & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b &= 3 + (-2) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186216835 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given conditions in turn.\n\n- Since the graph passes through the point $\\left(0, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$, we have that $f(0) = \\dfrac{5}{2}$. So, we have the following equation, which we solve for $d{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -{a}^{0}+d & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -1+d & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -1+d+1 & =\\frac{5}{2}+1 \\\\ d & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- Since $ad=\\dfrac{21}{2}$, using the fact that $d = \\dfrac{7}{2}$, we obtain the following equation, which we solve for $a{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ad & =\\frac{21}{2} \\\\ a\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right) & =\\frac{21}{2} \\\\ \\frac{7a}{2} & =\\frac{21}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{7a}{2} & =2\\cdot \\frac{21}{2} \\\\ 7a & =21 \\\\ \\frac{7a}{7} & =\\frac{21}{7} \\\\ a & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=3$."] [17592186216841 :question/worked-solution "To find the *smallest possible difference* between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$, we must find the difference between\n\n- the *smallest* possible mean of dataset $B$, and\n- the *largest* possible median of dataset $A$.\n\nThe difference we wish to find can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\min\\left(\\textrm{mean}(B)\\right) - \\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute each of these values in turn.\n\n- First, we find the smallest possible mean of dataset $B$. We start by writing down our dataset as a frequency table. Since we wish to find the *smallest possible value* of the mean, we record $x_{\\textrm{min}}$, the *smallest* possible value of $x$ for each group. Then, we calculate $f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$ for each row.The results are shown below.Value $(x)$Frequency $(f)$$x_{\\textrm{min}}$$f \\cdot x_{\\textrm{min}}$$55-57$$4$$55$$4\\cdot 55 = 220$$57-59$$6$$57$$6\\cdot 57 = 342$$59-61$$10$$59$$10\\cdot 59= 590$$61-63$$7$$61$$7\\cdot 61 = 427$$63-65$$3$$63$$3\\cdot 63 = 189$Therefore, the smallest possible value of the mean is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}min(\\text{mean}(B)) & =\\frac{220+342+590+427+189}{4+6+10+7+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1768}{30} \\\\  & \\approx 58.933\\end{aligned}\n$$\nrounded to three decimal places.\n- Next, we compute the largest possible median of $A{:}$Since the histogram for dataset $A$ is symmetric, the median must lie in the group $24-26$. Therefore, the smallest possible value of the median is\n$\\max\\left(\\textrm{median}(A)\\right) = 26$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that the smallest possible difference between the mean of $B$ and the median of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmin(mean(B)) - max(median(A)) &= 58.933 - 26.0 \\\\\n&\\approx 32.9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."] [17592186216848 :question/worked-solution "On the domain\n\n$$\nx\\ge 0\n$$\n\nand $a > 0$, an exponential $a\\cdot b^x+ c$ is\n\n- *decreasing* when $0 < b < 1$, with its maximum at $x=0$, and\n- *increasing* when $b>1$, and has no maximum.\n\nNow, consider each function in turn.\n\n- For the function\n$f(x)=18\\cdot(1.2)^x$,\nthe base is $1.2>1$. So, the function has no maximum. Hence, the equation of $f$ does *not* display the maximum as a constant term or a coefficient.\n- For the function $g(x)=27\\cdot(0.75)^x$, the base is $0.75\\in(0,1)$. So, the maximum occurs at $x=0$, and is equal to\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(0) & =27\\cdot (0.75)^{0} \\\\  & =27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus, the maximum value is $27$, which is exactly the *coefficient* shown. Hence, the equation of $g$ *does* display the maximum as a coefficient.\n- For the function $h(x)=9\\cdot(0.75)^x+4$, the base is $0.75\\in(0,1)$. So, the maximum occurs at $x=0$, and is equal to\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(0) & =9\\cdot (0.75)^{0}+4 \\\\  & =9+4 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus, the maximum value is $13$. However, neither the constant term ($4$) nor the coefficient ($9$) equals $13$. Hence, the equation of $h$ does *not* display the maximum as a constant term or a coefficient.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."] [17592186216859 :question/worked-solution "Notice that each table gives function values at $x=-2,0$, and $2$.\n\nTo determine which table is correct, we first express $y$ in terms of $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4x + 2y &= 12 \\\\\n2y &= 12 - 4x \\\\\ny &= 6 - 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we’ll analyze each of the given values of $x$ in turn.\n\n- For $x = -2{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6-2(-2) \\\\  & =6+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For $x = 0{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6-2(0) \\\\  & =6-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n- For $x = 2{:}$\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6-2(2) \\\\  & =6-4 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the filled-in table is the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\"><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</th><th data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</th></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-2$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$10$</td></tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\"><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$6$</td></tr>\n<tr><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td><td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n"] [17592186216874 :question/worked-solution "Since there are infinitely many solutions to the system, both equations have the same set of solutions $(x,y)$. Therefore, we only need to consider the solution set of one of the equations.\n\nLet's consider each answer choice in turn.\n\n- First, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(t, f(t))$. So, we set $x = t$ and solve the equation $3x+y = 6$ for $y$, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y & =6 \\\\ y & =-3x+6 \\\\ y & =-3t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, $(t,-3t+6)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n- Next, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(g(t), t)$. So, we set $y = t$ and solve the equation $3x+y = 6$ for $x$, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y & =6 \\\\ 3x & =-y+6 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}(-y+6) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}y+6 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis gives $\\left(-\\dfrac13t + 2, t\\right)$, which is *not* the same as $(3t-6,t)$. Hence, $(3t-6,t)$ is **NOT** a valid parametrization. $\\color{red}{\\times}$\n- Finally, we must construct a parametrization of the form $(2t, h(t))$. So, we set $x = 2t$ and solve the equation $3x+y = 6$ for $y$, as follows:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y & =6 \\\\ y & =-3x+6 \\\\ y & =-3(2t)+6 \\\\ y & =-6t+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHence, $(2t,-6t+6)$ is a valid parametrization. $\\color{green}{\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186216893 :question/problem "Two identical wooden blocks are each in the shape of a cylinder with a height of $12$ centimeters. The surface area of each block is $M$ square centimeters. When the two blocks are joined together by gluing one of their bases together, the resulting solid has a surface area of $1.2M$ square centimeters. What is the radius, in centimeters, of each circular base?\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\cdot (\\text{base area})+(\\text{lateral surface area}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi {r}^{2}+2\\pi rh\\end{aligned}\n$$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*"] [17592186216895 :question/problem "A cylinder has a height of $h$ and a base radius of $r$. The second cylinder has an identical base but is one-and-a-half times higher. The two cylinders are joined together by gluing one of their bases together. Find an expression for the surface area of the combined solid.\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the surface area $S$ of a cylinder is given by*\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\cdot (\\text{base area})+(\\text{lateral surface area}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi {r}^{2}+2\\pi rh\\end{aligned}\n$$\n*where $r$ is the base radius and $h$ is its height.*"] [17592186216952 :question/worked-solution "On the domain\n\n$$\nx\\ge 1\n$$\n\nand $a > 0$, an exponential $a\\cdot b^x+ c$ is\n\n- *decreasing* when $0 < b < 1$, and has **no** minimum, and\n- *increasing* when $b>1$, with its **minimum** at $x=1$.\n\nNow, consider each function in turn.\n\n- For the function\n$f(x)=8\\cdot(0.7)^x+2$,\nthe base is $0.7\\in(0,1)$. So, the function is decreasing on $x\\ge 1$ and has **no** minimum. Hence, the equation of $f$ does *not* display a minimum value.\n- For the function\n$g(x)=12\\cdot(1.4)^x-3$,\nthe base is $1.4>1$. So, the minimum occurs at $x=1$, and is equal to\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(1) & =12\\cdot (1.4)^{1}-3 \\\\  & =12\\cdot 1.4-3 \\\\  & =16.8-3 \\\\  & =13.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe minimum value is $13.8$, which is **not** equal to the coefficient ($12$) or the constant term $(-3)$. Hence, the equation of $g$ does *not* display the minimum.\n- For the function\n$h(x)=15\\cdot(1.5)^x-7.5$,\nthe base is $1.5>1$. So, the minimum occurs at $x=1$, and is equal to\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(1) & =15\\cdot (1.5)^{1}-7.5 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 1.5-7.5 \\\\  & =22.5-7.5 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThus, the minimum value is $15$, which is exactly the *coefficient* shown. Hence, the equation of $h$ *does* display the minimum as a coefficient.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [17592186216955 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can take out a factor of $-1$ from the factor $(2-x)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{1}{8}(2 - x)(x + 1)(x + 5) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{8}(x - 2)(x + 1)(x + 5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has $x$-intercepts at $x=-5,-1,2$.\n- $y \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$ and $y \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{8}(0-2)(0+1)(0+5) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (-2)\\cdot (1)\\cdot (5) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe corresponding graph (not drawn to scale) is shown below.\n\n![](images/b9/b929a17573109c8d063ec1d66ee5eda278bab6895530e08fad807ede7c363de8.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n-5 \\leq a \\leq -1\n$$\n\nThe corresponding interval on the $x$-axis is highlighted on the graph. Notice that in this interval, the graph of the cubic lies below the $x$-axis. So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\leq 0\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, only option III does **NOT** satisfy this condition."] [17592186216956 :question/worked-solution "First, we sketch the graph of the cubic. From the equation, we conclude the following:\n\n- The graph has $x$-intercepts at $x=-1,4$, where $x=4$ is a \"double root.\"\n- $y \\to -\\infty$ as $x \\to \\infty$ and $y \\to \\infty$ as $x \\to -\\infty$.\n- The $y$-intercept is\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-5(0+1)(0-4)^{2} \\\\  & =-5\\cdot (1)\\cdot (-4)^{2} \\\\  & =-80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding graph (not drawn to scale) is shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fc1351a5714f0b77c4509ef83efe205c45c2dc529f10d6831b1ad3a72f58f97c.png)\n\nWe are interested in the points $(a,b)$ whose $x$-coordinate $a$ satisfies\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq a \\leq 4\n$$\n\nThe corresponding interval on the $x$-axis is highlighted on the graph. Notice that in this interval, the graph of the cubic lies below the $x$-axis. So, we must have\n\n$$\nb \\leq 0\n$$\n\nAmong the given options, only $x=1$ (option III) does **NOT** satisfy this condition."]}
